WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.210
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.380
Good evening.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.910
My name is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.800
I'm an astrophysicist and
on the faculty at Yale

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.320
in the Departments of
Astronomy and Physics.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.760
I happen to serve as the current Director

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.290
of The Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:29.290
And I'm really excited to invite
you to this evening's talk.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.270
The title of our talk
is Mozart's Starling:

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.680
A Story of Art, Language,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.123
and the Human Relationship
with the Natural World.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.430
Before we start, I would
first like to say thanks

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.330
to Richard and Barbara Franke

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.990
for the generous support of this program

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.890
as well as many other initiatives at Yale.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.320
And I would also like to give a thank you

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.390
to the Connecticut Parrot Society

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.120
and the Mozart Society of America

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.715
for spreading the word about today's talk

00:01:03.715 --> 00:01:07.100
and opening up this opportunity

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.100
for everyone to listen to
Lyanda's wonderful talk

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.023
that I'm eagerly waiting to hear.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.290
And to introduce Lyanda and

00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:28.500
to talk about birding in general,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.780
we have our local international

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.780
expert evolutionary ornithologist,
Professor Rick Prum,

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.430
who incidentally was the inaugural

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.743
and previous director
of the Franke Program.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.600
Rick, as many of you know, Rick Prum,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.430
for those of you who might
have read his wonderful books,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.100
has done research on
diverse topics including

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.590
avian final genetics, behavior evolution,

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.756
evolution of aesthetics.

00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:06.400
And most of all, he is an avid birder.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.400
So I would like to turn over
hosting today's event to Rick.

00:02:12.547 --> 00:02:15.433
And Rick, welcome and thank you so much.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.257
- Thank you, Priya,

00:02:17.257 --> 00:02:20.430
and a pleasure to be here today

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.200
visiting with the Franke
Program in our new

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.433
or our accustomed Zoom state.

00:02:27.352 --> 00:02:31.375
First, thanks again to
Rich and Barbara Franke

00:02:31.375 --> 00:02:33.760
and great to see them here today.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.833
It's my pleasure to
introduce the speaker today.

00:02:37.751 --> 00:02:40.860
Lyanda Haupt is an award
award-winning author,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.960
naturalist, and eco philosopher

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.930
whose writing in multiple books

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:49.560
has connected nature and biodiversity

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.913
to the everyday lives of people.

00:02:53.295 --> 00:02:56.200
Her previous books include
The Urban Bestiary:

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.183
the Encountering of
Everyday Life in the Wild,

00:03:02.059 --> 00:03:06.630
Crow Planet: Essential Wisdom
for the Urban Wilderness,

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.510
and Pilgrim on the Great Bird Continent:

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.990
Importance of Everything and Other Lessons

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.470
from Darwin's Notebooks.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.650
All published by Little Brown.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.320
In the next month, she
has a new book coming out,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.350
Rooted: Life at the Crossroads
of Science, Nature and Spirit

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.550
with Little Brown.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.130
And today we have the
pleasure from hearing from her

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:32.130
on her award-winning book
in 2017, Mozart's Starling.

00:03:32.252 --> 00:03:35.750
So thank you and welcome to
the Franke Program, Lyanda.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.500
- Thank you so much.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.850
I'm delighted to be here.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.810
I want to begin with some gratitude.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.910
Thank you to the Frankes
for your generosity.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:48.740
I am so happy to be part
of this beautiful series.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.190
Thank you, Professor Natarajan.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.667
I'm sure I said your name incorrectly but.

00:03:54.322 --> 00:03:56.040
And thank you, Professor Prum,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.370
for that lovely introduction.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:01.310
And thank you to Professor
Ty Kamp for arranging this.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.230
We had so many emails back and forth

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.610
figuring out how things would go.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:10.040
And who else should I thank?

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.360
I know who.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.320
Frederick.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.240
Professor Kamp's son, Frederick,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.330
I hear started this whole adventure

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:19.480
with a thoughtful holiday gift.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.980
So well done, Frederick.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:21.813
Thank you.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.167
So--

00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:28.800
- Lyanda, I forgot to thank Carmen

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.100
for today's talk as well to be featured.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.730
So I wanted to say thank you

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.440
to both you, our wonderful speaker,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.870
and our participant, active
participant hopefully,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.690
Carmen, who will be there.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.230
And I also wanted to
give a shout out because

00:04:48.413 --> 00:04:50.610
to Rick's book, which is,

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:54.620
this is one of my absolute
favorite books as well.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.433
So I think for all of
you, without further ado,

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:01.610
Lyanda, please take it away.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:03.060
- One further delay, if I may, I'm sorry.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.100
I was remiss, I should have mentioned.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.020
Please put your questions
during the talk in the chat

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.120
and we'll monitor the chat.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.030
And at the end of the day,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.640
we'll get to as many of
those questions as we can.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:13.473
So thank you.

00:05:13.473 --> 00:05:15.453
And now.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:18.600
- Onward.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.270
Okay, yes, ask me anything.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.520
All right, I'm gonna
pop the share screen on.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.460
I wish that we were in
person together, of course.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.260
It's wonderful to be so.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.610
But one of the things that this platform

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.140
gives us the opportunity
to do is to gather

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:35.780
from places all over the place.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.010
And so I wanted to share briefly that I am

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.210
speaking to you from Seattle
in the Pacific Northwest.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.120
We are surrounded by the Cascade Mountains

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:44.750
and the Olympic Mountains.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.400
And this is really a land of

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.290
mountains and rivers without end.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.150
Lots of ferns and mosses.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.370
And just down the road from my house

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:58.630
is a wooded forested
path to the Salish Sea.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:00.470
And so I want to say at this point

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:03.620
that I live and work on the unceded lands

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.050
of the Duwamish Tribe and
the Coast Salish peoples.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.640
And I acknowledge with
honor and so much gratitude

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.250
this land and the people
who have lived here

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.663
from time immemorial.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.730
If you can see, I'm
gonna kind of pop this up

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:25.387
'cause we've got a bird coming through.

00:06:25.387 --> 00:06:28.390
I'm kind of laughing because the title

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:32.810
of this lecture series is the
Distinguished Speaker Series.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.830
And yet I am coming to you from my kitchen

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:39.710
with a starling on my
head and pandemic hair.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.920
And I'm feeling that the
whole kitchen situation

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.390
is very undistinguished.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.710
And I promise you that
upstairs in my study,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.800
there's lots of books,
high distinguished level.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.680
But the reason that we're here is that

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:57.440
Ty Kamp and I were
talking before this series

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.210
about whether or not Carmen could join

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.120
for the Q&amp;A afterwards.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.360
And I said, she's welcome to join the talk

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.790
and that would be fun but
she has a mind of her own

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:06.870
and there's no guaranteeing

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.120
that if we just open the door
of her aviary for the Q&amp;A

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.920
that she'll come out then.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:15.700
So I decided, we decided together,

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.360
to just have the door of her aviary open.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.420
It's really close by here.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.850
This is her most comfortable room.

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:23.870
So she might be flying in and out.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.270
And I think we might just be a
little bit light on our feet.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.550
And I might talk about
starling natural history

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.140
not necessarily in the
order that I had planned it

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:35.710
just so that we can take
advantage of having the bird here.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.710
So I usually start by doing a little poll.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.470
And I'm not gonna do the Zoom poll.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.300
I just would like you all to humor me

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:48.860
and from wherever you are,

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.430
if you are a person who
feels that you could,

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:54.880
with maybe 95% accuracy,

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.330
identify a starling when you see one,

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.503
please raise your hand.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.083
Okay.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.520
Lots.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.520
Lots of hands and not all the
hands, just as to be expected.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.620
So I will tell you that if
you did raise your hand,

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.810
you are really in the upper
just few percent strata

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.300
of people in this country.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.203
Most people do not know,

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.640
if they know anything about birds at all,

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.990
what they know about starlings is that

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:30.840
we are supposed to dislike them.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.450
But very few people can identify them.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:37.570
Many people think that
they are crows, baby crows,

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.930
conflating smallness with baby-ness

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:44.690
as people often do, or
blackbirds generally.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:47.990
I was speaking with a
very illustrious person

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:51.120
in the opera world when I
was researching this book.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.710
And we were sitting at
a coffee shop in Seattle

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.700
where he has lived for two decades.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:01.640
And he knew about that
Mozart had a starling

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.730
and we were talking a lot about that.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:06.080
And he finally came to a
point in the conversation

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.520
where he leaned over and he said,

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.040
but you know, I've never seen a starling.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:12.350
And I thought, oh my goodness.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:13.363
So I said,

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:19.173
Maestro, just lean over this way into the,

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:21.493
to the,

00:09:23.198 --> 00:09:25.700
and look at the wire above the coffee shop

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.160
and there are four
starlings sitting there.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:29.003
And he just laughed.

00:09:29.003 --> 00:09:30.010
I said, you're walking under them

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.230
every single day of your life.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:35.520
A question that I'm often
asked is why I wrote this book.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.663
And it's a question
that every author gets.

00:09:42.157 --> 00:09:45.440
It's more fitting for me with this book

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.980
because I am an
ecologically minded person.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:49.470
I'm a nature writer.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.030
I'm a student of bird life.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:54.730
And if there's one thing that
people know about starlings

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:58.080
is that they are the most
despised bird in North America,

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:59.220
for people that know anything

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.700
about conservation and ornithology,

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.940
and that's with good reason.

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:08.940
They cause $800 million of
agricultural damage every year.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:14.270
They are a non-native species
and an invasive species.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:17.130
So they have grown in from

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.500
when they were introduced here in 1890

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.620
from a population of just 80 birds

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.420
to the 200 million starlings

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.810
that now blanket North America.

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:31.750
And their main ecological
issue is that they compete with

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:33.270
native cavity nesters,

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:35.590
for that rare resource of cavity nests.

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.070
So small birds like chickadees,
certain woodpeckers,

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.070
blue birds and things like that.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:40.903
So.

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.990
People say why would I of all people

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:46.240
write a book about starlings?

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.360
And it's something that I
never thought I would do.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.010
I had already,

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:52.400
I apologize that the
starling is fluttering

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.540
through my already bad pandemic hair, so.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:56.520
It's kind of a

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:00.010
funny situation.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:01.003
It's okay, Carmen.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.890
So one day I was working

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.090
on a completely different book at my desk

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:10.970
and I looked out the
window at the parking strip

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.170
and what I saw was about, it was autumn,

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.140
and so the family groups of summer

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:17.400
were starting to break down

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.750
and gather into the larger flocks

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:24.755
that characterize the huge
gatherings of starlings

00:11:24.755 --> 00:11:27.560
in autumn and into the winter.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.250
And I was looking out there and I thought,

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.160
dang it, there's 30 starlings.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.660
They're scaring away the
cute little chickadees

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:35.890
that I would rather have out there.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.800
So I pounded on the window and the birds,

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.070
I had a teenager at the time,

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.810
so I recognized the
look that they gave me.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.760
It was just, it was complete indifference.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.290
They kind of looked up at me like,

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.370
yeah, we don't care what you think.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:49.970
And so I pounded harder.

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:52.520
And this time, the starlings
lifted from the grass

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.560
and they turned into the light,

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.340
and then of course they
didn't actually leave.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.453
They went back onto the sidewalk.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:01.330
But in that moment,

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:04.310
the light captured that
beautiful iridescence

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:05.730
of their feathers.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.470
And I thought, you know,
I might dislike starlings,

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:12.923
but dang, they are so, so pretty.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.040
And then I noticed it
seemed kind of serendipitous

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:17.693
that, at the same time,

00:12:17.693 --> 00:12:22.693
Spotify was on and Mozart's
Prague Symphony was playing.

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:26.120
And I didn't know at the
time that that was a symphony

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.190
that he composed during the time

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:30.011
that he lived with a starling.

00:12:30.011 --> 00:12:33.700
But I did sort of have that
sense of things coming together.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:36.583
And I remembered in that moment

00:12:36.583 --> 00:12:39.080
the story that I'd heard
over and over again

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.850
and even had written about
just briefly in earlier books,

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.340
that Mozart had a pet starling.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:48.247
And I sat there contemplating
that kind of dissonance.

00:12:48.247 --> 00:12:49.563
That here in,

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:54.600
here is this composer
that we consider to be

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.750
one of the most sublime voices

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:00.640
in the Western classical canon.

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.140
And he had, as a muse and a companion,

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:05.870
this most despised of birds.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.550
And I should point out that

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.913
in Europe, where Mozart is from,

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:15.610
starlings are native birds

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.510
and so they don't have the
reputation that they have here.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.160
So I sat there kind of dwelling

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.297
in that complicated dissonance

00:13:24.297 --> 00:13:26.190
and I just went down the rabbit hole

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:27.810
of exploring the story.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.630
And the thing that I
had to start with was,

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:31.810
was it even true?

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:32.900
Because there are so many

00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:36.070
apocryphal stories about composers.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:39.210
There's an idea that Beethoven
cut the legs off of his piano

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.970
because he was deaf and he
could sit on the floor and hear

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:46.970
the tremblings of movement or vibrations

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.270
of the piano while he played.

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:51.940
And that is very, very
likely an untrue story.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.260
And I thought, gosh,
maybe I've been misled

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:57.340
by this Mozart starling story.

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.450
But it turns out that it is true.

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:01.330
Did a little research and found out

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.900
that this is pretty much the way it went.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.620
In April of 1784,

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.480
Mozart was living in Vienna and

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:14.230
he just finished a new concerto.

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:17.840
It was the beautiful Concerto in G Major.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:20.140
And he was not very
good at keeping records

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:22.660
but one thing that he did
keep really good track of

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.190
was his finished music.

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:29.190
So he wrote in his composition diary

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.750
that he had completed this concerto.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.300
It was K.453.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:39.300
The 453rd finished piece that
he had written at age 24.

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:42.350
Makes me feel kind of lazy.

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:44.410
And there were hundreds
of others of smaller,

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:46.340
lesser compositions that he didn't think

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.420
worthy of making it into the diary.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.190
So anyway, April 1784.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:52.380
Wrote the concerto.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.820
He had a fair copy of it made

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:56.810
to send to his father, Leopold,

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:59.567
the great violinist and violin pedagogue

00:14:59.567 --> 00:15:04.493
who was living in Salzburg
about 250 miles away,

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:07.610
by kind of rough carriage road.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.490
So he had that sent to his
father along with a letter

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:13.610
that said, Father, I would
love your opinion about this.

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:16.550
He always valued Leopold's good opinion.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:18.800
But he said, please be
very careful with it.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.410
Don't let anyone see it.

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.500
Mozart was always a little bit paranoid

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.170
that his work was going to be stolen

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:30.010
or copied or imitated
by a lesser composer.

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.300
And not without reason.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:32.870
This happened sometimes.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.470
So this is all in April.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:37.180
Sent it to his father, and in
May, he got confirmation that

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:39.163
Leopold in Salzburg had received it.

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:41.790
This composition was slated

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:44.600
to be performed in June in Vienna.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:46.410
So April, it's finished.

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.060
June, it's going to be performed.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:54.560
In the middle in May, May 26th of 1784,

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.760
Mozart went into a bird shop,

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.610
and he lived in the Graben area of

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:04.140
that kind of fashionable
shopping district of Vienna,

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:05.410
then and now.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:08.040
And so he went downstairs
from his apartments

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:09.300
and he was walking the street.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.910
He walked into a bird vendor's shop

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:15.220
and he bought a starling
for three kreutzer.

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:20.030
And the starling was singing
the motif from the concerto,

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:23.540
from Allegretto movement,
of the Concerto in G.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:27.700
So we know this because,

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:30.720
although I said that Mozart
is a terrible record keeper,

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:33.930
he did for a while keep
a small expense notebook

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:37.770
where he wrote down everything
that he ever bought.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.580
And it's kind of funny.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.100
It's anomalous 'cause it wasn't something

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:43.440
that he would normally do.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.410
He was married to Constanze Weber Mozart,

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:51.940
who was very good at home economics.

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.430
She had to be because their economic

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:58.160
finances were in such disarray.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:00.160
So in my imagination,

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:03.210
it was her, Constanze,
that gave the notebook

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:05.100
to Mozart with the admonition,

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:07.580
honey, I want you to write
down every single thing

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:10.030
that you buy every day
and then show it to me

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:12.670
at the end of the week and we'll talk.

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:14.690
So Mozart was really good about that.

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:16.890
And he wrote down three
kreutzer for the bird

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:19.990
and right underneath it, he wrote down

00:17:22.850 --> 00:17:23.963
what he heard.

00:17:25.768 --> 00:17:29.130
So this is Mozart a couple
of years before this.

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:31.510
This is Mozart at about 22 years old.

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:33.713
He was 24 when he got the bird.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.770
And this is what he heard.

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:40.550
So what this slide does,

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:42.800
if you're not musical, don't worry.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.860
But it shows the line of the motif

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.770
that the starling was singing

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:50.360
and it shows it the way
that Mozart wrote it

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:52.250
and then the way the starling sang it.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.763
So the starling made a couple of changes.

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.550
If you're not musical, don't worry.

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:03.490
But there's this little
arch with the dot under it.

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:06.100
That's a fermata, a long pause.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:11.100
And then you'll also see that
the bird sharped the G's.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.750
Which sounds absolutely terrible,

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:17.350
but it's exactly the sort of
thing that a starling would do.

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:19.020
So I'm gonna play this for you.

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:21.410
And this is just me plunking it on

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.523
the piano in the living room.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:26.430
And if you listen carefully,

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:31.180
you'll be able to hear Carmen
from way over here in the,

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:33.640
kind of the mudroom where her aviary is,

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.463
singing in the background.

00:18:39.391 --> 00:18:40.224
So listen for the changes.

00:18:40.224 --> 00:18:42.850
Listen carefully to the first line.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.033
The starling does not
sing the grace notes.

00:18:46.806 --> 00:18:48.783
But here we go.

00:18:52.489 --> 00:18:57.489
(soft piano music)
(bird singing)

00:19:14.531 --> 00:19:18.300
I hope you could hear Carmen
in the background there.

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:20.980
So a lot of, most people,

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:22.720
most biographers of Mozart

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.010
do not mention the starling at all

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:26.700
because it takes some sleuthing

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:28.020
to get to the bottom of the story.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.370
You have to look at diaries, notebooks,

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:33.780
letters from visitors
and from Mozart himself,

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.900
and other ephemera.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:38.830
But when people do mention the starling,

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:40.330
and there's a lot of misinformation about

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:41.430
what the starling could sing

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.050
and what the starling couldn't sing,

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:46.560
when they come to the idea that Mozart

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:51.080
heard the starling mimicking his motif,

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:53.010
that he must've been furious.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:54.930
So starlings are gifted mimics.

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:57.830
So it wasn't really strange
that a starling could do this.

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:59.750
And I will talk about that,

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:02.291
more about that in a little bit.

00:20:02.291 --> 00:20:04.600
People thought he must've been furious

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:06.830
because he really was paranoid that

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:10.640
people were going to copy
and imitate his music.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:13.329
And here somehow, it
had gotten in the world

00:20:13.329 --> 00:20:16.050
enough that a bird, the
starling, a good mimic,

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:18.710
could learn to mimic it.

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:22.450
But the information that
we have from Mozart himself

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:24.670
in the little notebook that he kept

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:27.550
was the record that he bought the bird,

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:30.810
these two lines that
he copied, and we have,

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.900
Mozart had absolutely perfect pitch.

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:34.370
So we know that his version

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:37.370
of the starling song was exactly right.

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:38.670
And then underneath that,

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:41.120
instead of some exclamation of fury,

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:44.770
he wrote, and I, forgive
me, I do not speak German,

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:46.967
but the line is (speaking
in foreign language).

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:50.823
That was wonderful.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:54.703
So.

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.450
So we have the real Carmen here

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:03.400
and you guys will have to tell
me since I can't see myself,

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:07.310
if I'm situated well
enough that you guys can,

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:08.690
y'all can see her.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:10.310
Is this all right?

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:11.560
- [Ty] Yep, you're fine.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:14.130
- Okay good, so as you can see,

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:15.470
Carmen looks a little bit different

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:18.770
than the young bird she
was in that photograph.

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:22.777
One of the questions that
people often ask is how.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:27.860
They think that I talk
about this wonderful mystery

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:31.430
of Mozart coming to have the starling,

00:21:31.430 --> 00:21:33.730
and it's a multilayered mystery.

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:36.670
And one of the questions
that people ask me most is,

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:41.670
how did a starling learn
to sing this motif?

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:45.220
And how is really not
that much of a mystery.

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:47.290
Starlings are gifted mimics.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:49.230
And in this country especially,

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.940
in North America where
starlings are so despised,

00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:55.740
people bother to learn
very little about them.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:58.190
But they are better mimics

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:01.280
than the corvids, the
crows and jays and ravens.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.860
And they are on par with some parrots.

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:06.370
So it depends on the starling
and it depends on the parrot.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:10.020
(laughs) But they're very capable mimics

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:13.700
and they mimic environmental
sounds, other birds,

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:18.150
animals, music, and the human
voice and many other things.

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:22.940
So the real issue is when

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:26.490
the starling learned Mozart's concerto,

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:28.440
because as I said, he
finished it in April.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:30.610
It wasn't supposed to be out until June.

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:33.870
So we don't know if
Mozart visited the shop

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:36.690
and taught the bird

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.430
the motif himself when
he was whistling along,

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:40.770
as he often did,

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:45.320
or whether he heard Mozart
practicing out the window

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:47.250
from his apartments above,

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:49.770
or there are all kinds
of other possibilities

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:52.880
of the way that the starling
could have learned it.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.389
And in most, whenever,

00:22:54.389 --> 00:22:57.291
you'll read about this in program notes

00:22:57.291 --> 00:22:58.980
when you go to the symphony

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:00.910
and hear this concerto performed.

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:04.002
And I am absolutely shocked at how often

00:23:04.002 --> 00:23:09.002
the course of events described
are absolutely impossible.

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:10.430
Or people will say,

00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:12.310
learned people will say in program notes

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:15.530
that the starling could
sing the whole concerto,

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:17.530
which even for a starling is too much.

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:20.640
Or that the starling sang
a couple of notes flat,

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:24.371
which no respectable
starling would sing flat.

00:23:24.371 --> 00:23:27.470
(laughs) So one of the
things that I enjoyed doing

00:23:27.470 --> 00:23:29.420
was all the research that it really came

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:30.830
to narrow down the possibilities

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:32.763
of what could have actually happened.

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:39.810
So I really went down the rabbit hole

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.850
of exploring the relationship
with Mozart and his starling.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:46.710
I read every scientific
paper that I could find

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:50.410
in any language that I could
read, which is English.

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:52.493
So. (laughs)

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:55.280
I've read all of the
scientific literature.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:57.140
I put my binoculars around my neck

00:23:57.140 --> 00:24:00.440
and went out into the
urban wilds and studied

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.530
starlings extensively in the field.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:06.250
I traveled to Vienna and spent days

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:09.510
in the apartments where
Mozart lived with his starling

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:11.400
to kind of imagine how their life

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:14.210
would have gone together there.

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:16.380
And I realized that there was one thing

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:19.320
that was missing in all of this research

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:22.050
that I really needed to do

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:25.260
in order to understand how
Mozart lived with a starling.

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:27.663
And that was to take a drastic step.

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:34.380
And live with a starling myself. (laughs)

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:37.790
So let me first say

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:41.190
that it is absolutely illegal to

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:47.560
harm, touch, even glance
sideways at any nest, nestlings,

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:51.340
or most birds in North America.

00:24:51.340 --> 00:24:53.960
So it is completely illegal to have

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:56.450
a nestling bird of most kinds.

00:24:56.450 --> 00:24:59.420
Most birds are protected by
the Migratory Bird Treaty Act.

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:01.470
Starlings are an exception.

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:04.860
In being a non-native
bird and an invasive bird,

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:06.900
they are not protected by the Treaty Act

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:08.500
or Fish and Wildlife.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:13.060
And we are not only allowed
but actually encouraged

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:16.090
by federal and local Fish
and Wildlife departments

00:25:16.090 --> 00:25:19.360
to remove starling nests
and eggs and even nestlings

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:20.960
in any way that we can.

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:24.370
So I personally agree
that we should find ways

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:27.700
to limit starling nests and eggs.

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:29.630
But I personally ethically believe

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:32.000
that after the nestlings are hatched

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.040
is not the time to remove the nestlings.

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:38.050
I did, however, learn
when I was on the hunt

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:40.590
for a starling for my research

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:44.710
that there was a nest in Seattle Park,

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.130
not far from my home,

00:25:46.130 --> 00:25:48.360
that was gonna be removed
by the Fish and Wildlife

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.533
or the City Parks Department.

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:55.890
And I had an insider in the parks who knew

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:58.810
the research that I was working
on and he told me about it.

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:02.750
And I said, those starlings
have actually hatched already,

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:04.780
because I've been studying
all the starlings in the area.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:06.990
And he said, well, no one cares.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:08.120
They're just starlings.

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:10.613
So he said, it's gonna happen tonight.

00:26:10.613 --> 00:26:12.090
(laughs) You haven't had that much time.

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:17.090
So as with a lot of
so-called wildlife control,

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:22.510
when animals are removed by wildlife

00:26:22.510 --> 00:26:24.503
or killed by wildlife,

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.330
Fish and Wildlife organizations
or City Parks Department,

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:34.430
they do it at night so that
people don't get upset.

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:36.950
So I told my husband

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:38.800
that it was our one
chance to get a starling

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:40.960
and he was a little bit dubious about it

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:42.010
but we went up to the park.

00:26:42.010 --> 00:26:45.920
It was a harrowing
adventure that involved us

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:49.940
sneaking into the boys'
bathroom at a park,

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:51.940
borrowing the park's garbage can,

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:53.770
climbing on top of it.

00:26:53.770 --> 00:26:55.280
Starlings are cavity nesters

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:57.400
and they like to nest far enough back

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:00.350
that a raccoon's paw cannot reach them.

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:03.770
And in this case, the
starlings were way, way back

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:07.440
in the eaves of this kind of
slightly open aired bathroom.

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:10.100
So I climbed up there and I couldn't reach

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:12.310
and my husband climbed up
there with his long arm

00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:15.030
and he could just, we could feel the heat

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.050
of the starling nestlings but
we couldn't quite reach them.

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:20.420
And finally, with me sort of leaning

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:22.680
on this garbage can against the wall

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:25.820
to keep it from falling out
from under my poor husband

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:28.350
so he wouldn't end up
just dangling by his arm

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:31.740
from the boys' bathroom ceiling,

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:35.520
finally, he grasped the starling chick

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:37.810
and he dropped it in my hands.

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:39.800
And the chick was only a few days old.

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:42.880
And I looked at this
little bird and I thought,

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:46.720
oh my goodness, this bird is so sickly.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:47.990
She was wheezing.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:49.620
She had ectoparasites that I could

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:52.510
literally see crawling all over her.

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:56.900
And she just didn't seem,
she seemed highly ephemeral.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:59.910
And I said, honey, it would
be better if we had two.

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:01.560
They could keep each other warm.

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:05.260
And the glare I got from
my husband in that moment

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:09.020
with him standing there with
his bruised arm was just,

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:10.630
it was priceless but it also told me

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:13.143
that we were going to have
just the one starling.

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:14.983
Carmen.

00:28:16.290 --> 00:28:17.163
So.

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:21.570
What I didn't realize is that

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:25.720
I have a background in
rehabilitation of birds,

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:28.210
especially raptors, but at
the Raptor Center in Vermont,

00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:29.890
people would bring in all kinds of birds.

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:32.010
And so I'd raised starlings before

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:33.860
and many other young birds but

00:28:35.913 --> 00:28:38.350
I didn't realize that raising one

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:41.500
just from a few days to adulthood
would take over my life.

00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:44.630
And this is a video, and
we were testing it before

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:47.400
and we found out that it's just not,

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:48.680
it's not playing very well.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:49.950
It's a little bit glitchy.

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:52.630
But this is how she looked from

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:55.690
my writing desk.

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:57.650
So I would sit every day,

00:28:57.650 --> 00:28:59.970
writing this book, Mozart's Starling.

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:02.690
My laptop is in the middle of my desk.

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:05.780
To the right was this little
tiny Tupperware with this

00:29:06.970 --> 00:29:11.300
kind of frightful, getting
uglier by the day, baby starling

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:13.120
and hungrier as well,

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:15.320
who needed feeding every

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:18.630
20 minutes from dawn to dusk.

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:21.530
And on the other side of my computer,

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:23.570
there was often Delilah the cat.

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:24.470
And it was really funny.

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:29.000
Delilah knew that she wasn't
supposed to eat the bird.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:32.280
But she would ever so slightly

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:34.010
reach over with her paw.

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:35.832
And I'd say, Delilah, nope.

00:29:35.832 --> 00:29:37.190
And she'd pull it back and just pretend

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:38.313
to be licking herself.

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:43.970
And I'm just gonna put
this on just for a second

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:45.530
and see if you can get a sense of it.

00:29:45.530 --> 00:29:47.651
And I'm not gonna leave
it on for the whole time

00:29:47.651 --> 00:29:48.790
'cause I know it's not really gonna work,

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:50.460
but I'm just gonna keep my fingers crossed

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:53.293
and see if you can get
a little bit of a vibe.

00:29:54.693 --> 00:29:55.610
Here, baby.

00:29:56.452 --> 00:29:57.285
Just from my end.

00:29:58.153 --> 00:30:00.820
(bird chirping)

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:09.480
So I'm sorry if that
didn't look good for you,

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:13.540
but it's just kind of a fun little image

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:18.410
of the cheeping bird that
was my constant company.

00:30:18.410 --> 00:30:19.960
What was really funny about this

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:22.400
and what I didn't expect was how limiting

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:25.380
this would be to my movement.

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:27.030
I had to be,

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.300
I did have to feed her every 20 minutes

00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:30.930
and keep her a certain temperature.

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:33.040
I basically couldn't leave the house.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:35.600
If I went for a walk and I was back,

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:38.260
it took me 22 minutes instead of 20,

00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:41.750
I would walk inside and I
would hear this incredible

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:44.300
churring sound.

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:46.180
There's this very loud churring sound

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:47.790
that young starlings make.

00:30:47.790 --> 00:30:49.820
They outgrow it in a couple of months.

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:53.390
But it would just be pouring
over the whole house.

00:30:53.390 --> 00:30:55.370
And I would rush up to feed her.

00:30:55.370 --> 00:30:57.090
And one time I thought,

00:30:57.090 --> 00:30:59.950
I'm tired of being stranded
and I need groceries.

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:01.660
And so I packed her up in her box,

00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:03.480
I packed her up with the food,

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:06.370
and I just went to the
nearest grocery store.

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:07.560
And by the time I got there,

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:10.730
she was so cold 'cause she
needs to be about 85 degrees.

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:12.630
I had a lamp for her at home.

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:15.000
And so what I had to do
is I literally took her

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:16.787
and put her in my bra,

00:31:16.787 --> 00:31:20.160
(laughs) ran in, got my milk and bread,

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:21.130
came out again and thought,

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:23.393
all right, I'm never doing this again.

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:29.640
And you can see this
little stirring stick here

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:32.300
in the slide.

00:31:32.300 --> 00:31:34.040
One of the things that's interesting

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:35.700
about keeping a baby starling is that

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:38.940
although we are allowed to do anything

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:41.580
that we want to starlings,
kill and maim them,

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:44.430
we are not legally
allowed to take them home

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:45.520
and raise them in our living rooms.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:47.520
They don't want people
having pet starlings

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:49.110
that they might propagate.

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:51.510
So I was already a minor felon

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.150
in terms of taking the bird,

00:31:53.150 --> 00:31:54.720
but then I ran into Starbucks

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:57.940
and stole a whole handful of
those little stirring sticks

00:31:57.940 --> 00:31:58.900
to feed her with.

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:01.973
So my rap sheet was just getting
really long at this point.

00:32:05.386 --> 00:32:07.710
And even though she was
so sickly and wheezing,

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:10.730
with a lot of care, Carmen grew very fat.

00:32:10.730 --> 00:32:12.993
She did get uglier before she got cuter.

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:17.630
We couldn't tell for certain whether

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:19.923
she was a male or female at this point.

00:32:21.802 --> 00:32:23.070
So we chose the name Carmen,

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:26.110
which could be used for males or females.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:28.280
I love this juvenile plumage.

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.380
They have this very white chin

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:34.000
that allows them to be found from below,

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:36.230
which we discovered when

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:39.240
Carmen flew out the kitchen
window one day and she got lost

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:40.550
and we found her high, high

00:32:40.550 --> 00:32:42.650
in a fir tree outside our home.

00:32:42.650 --> 00:32:44.820
Partly by a contact call
that she was making,

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:47.600
but partly by the whiteness of her chin.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:50.550
So this is a little kind
of family photo album.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:55.300
Quickly, Carmen became
so at home among us.

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:58.720
And I don't know if she thinks that

00:32:58.720 --> 00:33:01.180
we are starlings or that she is

00:33:01.180 --> 00:33:02.480
and we're part of her flock

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.806
or she's part of our human family.

00:33:04.806 --> 00:33:07.530
Either way, she is very much at home.

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:09.320
And so this is kind of a little album

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:10.670
of how things look at home.

00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:14.270
This is a look at a
plumage that we rarely see

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:18.180
because the birds only keep
it for a couple of weeks,

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:20.880
and it's a time when they are cared for

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:23.280
and still pretty hidden by their parents.

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:27.093
So I like throwing this
in for bird people.

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:31.670
Claire, my daughter, is reading Jane Eyre

00:33:31.670 --> 00:33:33.930
and Carmen loved to sit on her shoulder

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.723
and kind of snuggle under her
hair while she was reading.

00:33:39.500 --> 00:33:42.829
And here she is all
grown into her beautiful

00:33:42.829 --> 00:33:45.453
teenage juvenile plumage.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.030
So this is what it looked like

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:50.070
and what it often looks like for me

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:53.040
to be researching or
writing on any given day.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:56.370
Carmen does like to be up with me.

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:59.000
And the reason that I didn't take her up

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.440
into my study today is
that sometimes she decides

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:03.140
all of a sudden that
she's anxious up there

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:04.010
and wants to leave.

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:06.010
And I didn't want to interrupt our talk.

00:34:06.890 --> 00:34:07.920
But as you can see,

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:09.770
I'm just noticing in this photo,

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:14.083
there's a lot of books
reflected in the window.

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:17.340
So you can see that it actually

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:20.380
is very distinguished up there. (laughs)

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:22.090
Anyway, Carmen would sit on my shoulder

00:34:22.090 --> 00:34:25.160
and she makes every single task I do

00:34:25.160 --> 00:34:27.893
take twice as long as it normally would.

00:34:29.979 --> 00:34:33.340
She steals my post-its.

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:35.150
She gets into all kinds of trouble.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:37.880
She sits on my hands
while I'm trying to type

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:39.830
and just creates all kind of a ruckus

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:42.773
but does like to be with me
no matter what I'm doing.

00:34:44.170 --> 00:34:46.700
She sends messages that
I'm not ready to send.

00:34:46.700 --> 00:34:49.853
She likes Facebook posts
that I do not like.

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:52.900
It's been a little bit of a problem.

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:55.660
But all of that I think looks
really kind of tranquil,

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:58.540
like it's fun having a
starling around, and it is,

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:02.500
but what it doesn't show is the background

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:08.420
symphony of starling sound
that we live with in our home.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:12.320
So I hope that this works.

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:15.013
I want to talk a little bit
about what you're gonna hear.

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:19.870
So in this video,

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:23.360
Carmen has two different
kinds of vocalizations.

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:28.080
One is the array of starling,

00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:30.230
just typical starling vocalizations.

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:34.853
And for people who are interested
in birds and ornithology,

00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:37.870
starlings are a songbird

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:40.530
and it is typical for most species that

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:44.803
the male of the species is
the singer and sings the,

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:48.630
I mean, males and females
both have many vocalizations,

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:51.970
but it's the male that
typically sings the species,

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:54.100
what is recognized as the species song

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:56.580
in the breeding season for many reasons.

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:58.160
In part to attract a mate,

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:01.913
to create and defend a
territory and other things.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:05.460
For a few birds, and
starlings are among them,

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:07.330
females also sing.

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:10.890
So male and female
starlings sing year round.

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:13.610
They don't just use their
chatty vocalizations.

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:16.330
They use actual song year round.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:21.280
It becomes more frequent and more animated

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:24.300
for male birds during the breeding season.

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:26.240
And it is also during the breeding season

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:30.510
that female starlings fall
absolutely silent on their nest.

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:32.320
The first time this happened with Carmen,

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:34.760
I thought that she was
depressed or that there was,

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:36.490
I was doing something wrong.

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:38.540
But I've learned that
she does it seasonally.

00:36:38.540 --> 00:36:43.540
And so it is little wonder
that it was only recently,

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:46.170
I mean, it was a little
bit over 10 years ago,

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:50.233
that we finally figured out
that female starlings also sing.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:55.340
So in this little clip,

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:57.960
you'll hear starling vocalizations,

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:01.540
but mixed in, you'll
hear some of that mimicry

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:04.120
that characterizes the talents,

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:06.453
the mimicry talents of the starling.

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:11.020
What's in here?

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:13.090
So you're gonna hear,
'cause it's a little,

00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:14.600
it's all a little bit whistly,

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:18.710
but you'll hear the whir
of the coffee grinder.

00:37:18.710 --> 00:37:21.150
You'll hear the creak of our oak floor.

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:24.280
Once Carmen learns new
sounds all of the time,

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:26.980
even though she is almost seven years old,

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:29.420
starlings, again, this is
different for starlings

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:30.770
from other songbirds.

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:34.136
Most songbirds usually use
learn their species song

00:37:34.136 --> 00:37:35.870
and other vocalizations within

00:37:35.870 --> 00:37:38.453
the first year or so of their life.

00:37:38.453 --> 00:37:40.290
And then that's it.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:42.440
But starlings, like some other mimics,

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:43.580
are lifelong learners.

00:37:43.580 --> 00:37:46.110
So they will actually mimic novel sounds

00:37:46.110 --> 00:37:48.600
and continue learning
throughout their lives.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:50.240
And Carmen's working on a new sound now.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:51.830
I'm trying to figure out what it is.

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:52.860
And this happens a lot.

00:37:52.860 --> 00:37:55.200
If she's not imitating or mimicking

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:58.000
a human vocalization or a piece of music,

00:37:58.000 --> 00:37:59.460
it'll be some environmental sound

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:01.500
and I cannot figure it out.

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:04.064
And so one of those was this sound

00:38:04.064 --> 00:38:06.636
where she goes (sings).

00:38:06.636 --> 00:38:08.330
And I thought, what is going on?

00:38:08.330 --> 00:38:10.200
And finally I stepped outside

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:13.950
just over the threshold of
where she usually hangs out,

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:17.180
and I stepped on our old wood oak floor

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:20.315
and it creaked in just
that same way. (sings)

00:38:20.315 --> 00:38:22.510
And I thought, she's
just mimicking the place

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:23.793
that I step every day.

00:38:24.930 --> 00:38:26.700
Also in here in quick succession,

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:29.860
you'll hear a meow, a hi
honey, and a kissy sound

00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:32.380
like that (puckers lips)
that you use to call animals,

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:34.090
and also a come here, honey.

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:36.660
So I'll play it, I hope that
you'll be able to hear it,

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:39.210
and I'll kind of point out
the sounds as they come.

00:38:40.679 --> 00:38:43.429
(bird whistling)

00:38:51.890 --> 00:38:53.090
That was coffee grinder.

00:38:54.408 --> 00:38:57.158
(bird whistling)

00:39:09.511 --> 00:39:10.858
- What?
- Well, you're not

00:39:10.858 --> 00:39:12.941
doing all the school for.

00:39:16.937 --> 00:39:19.604
- [Girl] What? It's just a bird.

00:39:23.351 --> 00:39:26.101
(bird whistling)

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:32.355
- Carmen is responding to that.

00:39:32.355 --> 00:39:35.105
(bird whistling)

00:39:53.833 --> 00:39:57.000
So I hope people could hear that okay.

00:39:59.534 --> 00:40:00.367
So.

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:04.140
Carmen uses her mimicry

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:06.870
in three different ways
that I have noticed.

00:40:06.870 --> 00:40:11.570
One of them is that she just
mixes it in like she did here.

00:40:11.570 --> 00:40:14.150
So she's making a lot of
typical starling noises

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:18.690
and she throws some of her
favorite mimic sounds inside.

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:21.100
She also does it randomly.

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:24.830
So I will be sitting in the
living room by myself, say,

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:26.350
and she'll be in her aviary.

00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:28.240
She goes into her aviary for the evening.

00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:29.240
We like to leave it open

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:32.110
so she can get as much
freedom of flight as possible.

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:34.820
But in the evening she starts
sort of bunking around.

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:39.090
So we make sure she's safe
inside when dusk falls.

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:40.290
But I will be in the living room

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:43.878
and I'll hear her say
just from the next room,

00:40:43.878 --> 00:40:45.183
come here, honey.

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:47.430
And I'll sit there going,

00:40:48.660 --> 00:40:50.856
am I supposed to go there?

00:40:50.856 --> 00:40:53.720
(laughs) This is sort of,
it's a little bit haunting

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:56.560
and kind of super interesting.

00:40:56.560 --> 00:40:59.640
But the other way is participatory.

00:40:59.640 --> 00:41:02.530
And I'm sorry I don't
have the slide for you.

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:07.180
It was a, I don't know what happened but

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:10.970
I'll at least tell you the story.

00:41:10.970 --> 00:41:13.480
So every morning when I come downstairs,

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:15.080
I'm usually the first one awake,

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:17.840
I'll come downstairs and
Carmen will look at me

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:19.960
and the first thing that she'll say,

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:24.210
in this hushed whispered
voice is, hi Carmen.

00:41:24.210 --> 00:41:26.130
Because that's what she usually hears

00:41:26.130 --> 00:41:28.470
when I come down the stairs first thing.

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:31.670
And then our big cat Delilah
will come down the stairs

00:41:31.670 --> 00:41:33.430
and Carmen will look at Delilah,

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:36.200
and before Delilah says
anything, Carmen says, (meows).

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:39.000
And then when I go and I pick up

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:41.480
the jar of coffee beans to make the coffee

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:45.380
but before I start the coffee
grinder, she says, (whirs).

00:41:46.620 --> 00:41:48.930
And then, and this is the
video I hope to show you,

00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:51.130
when I open and close
the door of the microwave

00:41:51.130 --> 00:41:55.380
but before I press the buttons,
she'll say, beep beep beep.

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:57.480
And it is pitch perfect.

00:41:57.480 --> 00:42:01.120
We literally cannot tell them apart.

00:42:01.120 --> 00:42:03.770
We cannot tell the microwave from Carmen.

00:42:03.770 --> 00:42:08.760
And so it took me months
of living with a starling

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:11.800
who had this wonderful aural attunement

00:42:11.800 --> 00:42:14.990
to recognize something
that is little understood

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:19.310
and little studied or
discussed in the literature.

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:21.200
And that is that Carmen,

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:22.460
starling vocalizations,

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:24.990
now I have no idea what's
going on with my hair

00:42:24.990 --> 00:42:29.453
after all that starling
visitation. (laughs)

00:42:31.990 --> 00:42:36.990
Starlings' aural involvement
with their environment

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:43.830
is not only participatory,
but it is anticipatory.

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:48.930
So she is always in the world, waiting,

00:42:48.930 --> 00:42:51.860
aurally attuned to what is going on

00:42:51.860 --> 00:42:54.620
and ready to participate by proclaiming

00:42:54.620 --> 00:42:57.263
the sound that is about to happen.

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:01.880
And this is, I just think,
the most magnificent knowledge

00:43:01.880 --> 00:43:03.720
and it took me so long to figure it out.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:05.810
And then to realize, my first thought was,

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:08.740
wow, I have a really smart
starling in my house.

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:10.940
But of course, it's
not just this starling.

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:12.970
It is a capacity of all starlings

00:43:12.970 --> 00:43:15.920
to live with this wild aural attunement.

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:17.850
And so when I step through the door,

00:43:17.850 --> 00:43:19.520
it makes me realize,

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:21.740
just from learning from this little bird,

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:24.750
makes me realize that we live within this

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:27.440
infinity of consciousness.

00:43:30.163 --> 00:43:33.630
Okay, I'm not even, consciousnesses.

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:34.870
There we go.

00:43:34.870 --> 00:43:37.630
An infinity of intelligences

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:41.290
that is beyond our knowing
and even our imagining.

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:44.620
And it makes the world
seem so full of wonder.

00:43:44.620 --> 00:43:46.180
I think of Emerson's quote,

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:49.943
we lie in the lap of an
immense intelligence.

00:43:51.060 --> 00:43:52.053
And so,

00:43:56.204 --> 00:43:58.690
I just think it's one of
the most wonderful things

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:00.480
I've learned from living with a starling.

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:02.040
And when I think about it,

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:04.710
I know that we have ecological
issues with the starling

00:44:04.710 --> 00:44:06.140
but I think this,

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:09.540
what we are learning from their
song and their vocalizations

00:44:09.540 --> 00:44:11.370
is so magnificent.

00:44:11.370 --> 00:44:12.603
What could be wrong?

00:44:16.787 --> 00:44:18.610
(humming)

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:21.630
I don't know if you can
see, I can't see myself.

00:44:21.630 --> 00:44:25.063
Enter Noam Chomsky.

00:44:30.330 --> 00:44:33.010
Shout out to Steve Harrington

00:44:33.010 --> 00:44:37.280
who has a company in Portland

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:39.630
called Just Say Gnome.

00:44:39.630 --> 00:44:42.640
He read my book and he
learned that I borrowed

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:45.830
the statue of garden Noam
Chomsky from a friend,

00:44:45.830 --> 00:44:47.833
and he sent me my own.

00:44:48.748 --> 00:44:49.873
So very happy.

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:54.660
So back in the 1950s,

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:58.120
this very bright young
linguist, Noam Chomsky,

00:44:58.120 --> 00:44:59.920
was hanging out at MIT,

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:03.930
thinking a lot about what
makes human language unique.

00:45:03.930 --> 00:45:07.980
And you have to remember that in the '50s,

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:09.180
behaviorism was very popular

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:11.810
and BF Skinner had the prominent

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:16.630
theory about how children learn language.

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:19.060
And he felt that it was through rewards.

00:45:19.060 --> 00:45:21.640
So if a kid says something right,

00:45:21.640 --> 00:45:23.950
the mom give him a pat on the head

00:45:23.950 --> 00:45:25.100
or a cupcake or something,

00:45:25.100 --> 00:45:27.530
and they get all of this
positive reinforcement.

00:45:27.530 --> 00:45:30.200
And Noam Chomsky thought, and rightly,

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:31.463
that that was nonsense.

00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:35.130
He came up over years
with a very different idea

00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:36.980
that we have a rule-based,

00:45:36.980 --> 00:45:40.060
innate rule-based way of

00:45:40.060 --> 00:45:43.610
coming to apprehend and utilize language.

00:45:43.610 --> 00:45:46.620
He called it universal grammar.

00:45:46.620 --> 00:45:49.480
And one of the most important aspects

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:52.000
of this universal grammar or rules

00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:54.260
was a certain kind of syntax.

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:56.340
So as humans, there's two different ways

00:45:56.340 --> 00:46:00.000
that we add information and
complexity to our sentences.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.940
One of them is by just

00:46:05.990 --> 00:46:07.910
adding onto it,

00:46:07.910 --> 00:46:09.660
adding onto the end of the sentence.

00:46:09.660 --> 00:46:13.940
So say we have a sentence
like, Mozart played the violin.

00:46:13.940 --> 00:46:15.040
We could add to it by saying,

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:18.680
Mozart played the violin
and had a pet bird.

00:46:18.680 --> 00:46:21.060
Mozart played the violin
and had a pet bird

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:22.820
who liked to sit on his shoulder.

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:24.370
And we could keep doing that forever.

00:46:24.370 --> 00:46:25.440
And for some weird reason,

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:26.837
the metaphor that I used in the book

00:46:26.837 --> 00:46:29.280
was a rat growing a longer tail.

00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:32.280
And for some other weird
reason, I didn't edit it out.

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:33.980
I still use that metaphor.

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:35.980
So the rat's tail is
growing longer and longer,

00:46:35.980 --> 00:46:39.280
adding onto it brick by
brick, check by check.

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:44.280
But the other way that humans
complexify our sentences

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:46.320
is from the inside out,

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:49.700
through a syntactical
device called recursion.

00:46:49.700 --> 00:46:52.730
So when we say, instead of saying,

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:57.730
Mozart played the violin
and had a pet starling,

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:02.200
we could say, Mozart,
who played the violin,

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:03.680
had a pet starling.

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:07.870
Mozart, who played the
violin and enjoyed singing,

00:47:07.870 --> 00:47:09.655
had a pet starling.

00:47:09.655 --> 00:47:12.230
And we could grow that
sentence from within

00:47:12.230 --> 00:47:13.840
over and over again forever.

00:47:13.840 --> 00:47:15.670
So instead of being like
the rat with a tail,

00:47:15.670 --> 00:47:17.960
it's our sentences become like peonies.

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:21.070
These layered, blossoming from within.

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:23.500
And we see recursion
sometimes in music as well,

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:26.010
including the music of Mozart.

00:47:26.010 --> 00:47:29.710
But this became very
important for Noam Chomsky,

00:47:29.710 --> 00:47:33.240
who decided that it was

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:38.410
a cornerstone of his universal grammar

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:43.410
that all humans and only humans

00:47:43.700 --> 00:47:45.490
recognize recursivity

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:50.370
in the way that we use our language.

00:47:50.370 --> 00:47:52.880
So anthropologists are actually busy

00:47:52.880 --> 00:47:54.350
debunking the first part of that,

00:47:54.350 --> 00:47:58.050
that all humans recognize recursion.

00:47:58.050 --> 00:48:00.487
But the really interesting
thing is that starlings

00:48:00.487 --> 00:48:03.720
are the first animal to prove

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:07.090
the second part of
Chomsky's theory incorrect.

00:48:07.090 --> 00:48:10.110
So there is a neuropsychologist,

00:48:10.110 --> 00:48:14.350
Tim Gentner, at UC San Diego.

00:48:14.350 --> 00:48:17.170
And he did some studies where he took

00:48:17.170 --> 00:48:21.430
starling sounding voices,
little bits of them,

00:48:21.430 --> 00:48:23.180
and he made them into patterns.

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:26.820
He made them into linear
patterns like AABB

00:48:26.820 --> 00:48:29.530
and then he made them
into recursive patterns

00:48:29.530 --> 00:48:31.240
where he'd stick a different pattern

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:33.890
in the middle of this linear pattern.

00:48:33.890 --> 00:48:37.090
And what he found was that
although it took some doing,

00:48:37.090 --> 00:48:41.770
it took many of the starlings
thousands of tries listening,

00:48:41.770 --> 00:48:46.460
that they could ultimately
recognize recursive patterns.

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:47.293
So.

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:53.500
When Noam Chomsky heard this
research, he was furious.

00:48:54.010 --> 00:48:56.350
He woke from his dogmatic slumbers

00:48:56.350 --> 00:48:58.690
and he rushed to critique the study.

00:48:58.690 --> 00:49:01.570
And he denounced the starling's behavior

00:49:01.570 --> 00:49:06.210
as being nothing more than
memorizing or counting rattles.

00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:08.590
He said there was basically party tricks.

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:10.550
And actually the things
that he was suggesting

00:49:10.550 --> 00:49:11.810
the starlings were doing were

00:49:11.810 --> 00:49:14.750
very complex behaviors for
a bird like a starling.

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:17.270
But he was unwilling to entertain the idea

00:49:17.270 --> 00:49:20.050
that they were recognizing recursion.

00:49:20.050 --> 00:49:23.940
And so later, Tim Gentner took great pains

00:49:23.940 --> 00:49:26.900
to make his studies more complexified

00:49:26.900 --> 00:49:29.290
and make the patterns more difficult

00:49:29.290 --> 00:49:31.640
so that there was really no way to claim

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:34.290
that the starlings were
just acting from memory

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:36.800
or counting.

00:49:36.800 --> 00:49:37.703
So.

00:49:39.360 --> 00:49:41.150
Yeah, you really have to side

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:43.230
with the starling science on that,

00:49:43.230 --> 00:49:44.950
which is quite fascinating.

00:49:44.950 --> 00:49:48.460
So linguistics were one of the ways,

00:49:48.460 --> 00:49:50.910
one of the main ways that
I ended up, to my surprise,

00:49:50.910 --> 00:49:53.660
I didn't expect to go down
the path of linguistics.

00:49:53.660 --> 00:49:56.660
By the way, I found myself on
the screen here, so thank you.

00:50:00.142 --> 00:50:01.323
It's the way that we're arranged.

00:50:03.270 --> 00:50:06.240
Did not expect to go down
the path of linguistics.

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:08.940
And we can talk more about
that if you want to in the Q&amp;A.

00:50:08.940 --> 00:50:11.250
There's just so much fun stuff there

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:13.630
about the anthropological
dimension as well.

00:50:13.630 --> 00:50:17.400
I did expect to go into music.

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:20.310
And one of the things
that surprised me was

00:50:20.310 --> 00:50:23.970
I've discovered the work of a
very well-known neurophysicist

00:50:23.970 --> 00:50:26.550
and she said the most remarkable thing.

00:50:26.550 --> 00:50:28.240
She said, I want to get the quote right.

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:33.240
That only humans have the
capacity to engage with music.

00:50:34.490 --> 00:50:37.350
Only humans have the capacity
to engage with music.

00:50:37.350 --> 00:50:40.040
And I read that aloud at
our dinner table one night

00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:41.440
and everyone just cracked up

00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:46.440
because we live with this
starling who is, who cannot,

00:50:46.730 --> 00:50:49.690
we cannot have music going on in the house

00:50:49.690 --> 00:50:53.070
without her reacting wildly.

00:50:53.070 --> 00:50:58.070
And I had this great fantasy

00:50:58.200 --> 00:50:59.580
when Carmen came to the house.

00:50:59.580 --> 00:51:04.580
I didn't want to teach her
any particular vocalization.

00:51:04.880 --> 00:51:07.310
I just wanted to see how her

00:51:07.310 --> 00:51:09.470
mimicry and vocal learning would unfold

00:51:09.470 --> 00:51:14.310
in the general household
round of our daily life.

00:51:14.310 --> 00:51:17.453
But I did have one
exception and that is that,

00:51:18.713 --> 00:51:20.133
I'm so sorry, I was just
making sure she's not

00:51:20.133 --> 00:51:23.103
bathing in the cat water
which is bad for her.

00:51:24.156 --> 00:51:26.210
She's bathing in some other water

00:51:26.210 --> 00:51:27.360
that she's not allowed to be in,

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:30.390
but it's safe at least.

00:51:30.390 --> 00:51:33.470
So the exception was that I was hoping

00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:36.870
she would learn to sing or mimic

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:40.980
the line that Mozart's starling sang,

00:51:40.980 --> 00:51:44.490
that from his Allegretto
from the Concerto in G Major.

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:48.720
So I put it on loop on my phone

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:50.530
and I played it for
her over and over again

00:51:50.530 --> 00:51:54.870
while she was growing up and
she had no interest whatsoever.

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:56.100
And here I'm thinking it would be

00:51:56.100 --> 00:51:57.990
the perfect conclusion to the book

00:51:57.990 --> 00:52:01.350
and she'd be famous and Oprah would come.

00:52:01.350 --> 00:52:03.530
But she just doesn't give it a glance.

00:52:03.530 --> 00:52:07.680
But other music, she leaps to life

00:52:07.680 --> 00:52:10.327
like an opera hero when she hears it.

00:52:10.327 --> 00:52:12.310
And she doesn't like
much of Mozart at all.

00:52:12.310 --> 00:52:14.490
She does like the theme of Papageno,

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:18.120
this very birdlike character
from The Magic Flute

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:20.930
that I believe to be inspired in some part

00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:23.280
by Mozart's time with this starling.

00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:26.040
She also likes one of his Divertimento,

00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:27.610
A Musical Joke,

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:30.350
that he wrote during the
time that she lived with

00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:32.880
or that his starling
lived with him as well.

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:37.610
And the famous ornithologist, Baptista,

00:52:37.610 --> 00:52:39.900
and an ethologist, Meredith West,

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:44.900
sort of over-played the Musical Joke

00:52:45.140 --> 00:52:47.590
overlain with the starling vocalizations,

00:52:47.590 --> 00:52:50.240
and definitely found the
vocal imprint of the starling

00:52:50.240 --> 00:52:53.990
on this piece of Mozart's
that has for so long

00:52:53.990 --> 00:52:57.290
been despised by musicians everywhere

00:52:57.290 --> 00:52:59.160
because it makes them sound so discordant

00:52:59.160 --> 00:53:00.830
and like they don't
know what they're doing.

00:53:00.830 --> 00:53:03.500
But it seems like Mozart knew
exactly what he was doing.

00:53:03.500 --> 00:53:07.250
He was composing a kind of
musical eulogy for his bird.

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:10.080
Anyway, other than those
couple of exceptions,

00:53:10.080 --> 00:53:12.940
Carmen unfortunately has
little interest in Mozart.

00:53:12.940 --> 00:53:17.940
But she loves Bach and
she loves bluegrass.

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:19.480
And she was fortunate with Bach

00:53:19.480 --> 00:53:21.940
because Claire, my
daughter, is a fine cellist.

00:53:21.940 --> 00:53:25.040
And so we had the
unaccompanied cello squeaks

00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:26.410
going on in the house a lot

00:53:26.410 --> 00:53:28.680
and Carmen would sing along with them.

00:53:28.680 --> 00:53:31.260
And my husband happens
to be a bluegrass fan

00:53:31.260 --> 00:53:33.970
and this is how we discovered
Carmen's love of bluegrass.

00:53:33.970 --> 00:53:37.600
And she even has a favorite
band, Greensky Bluegrass,

00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:38.490
and I think it's because they have

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:40.380
a really strong mandolin line

00:53:40.380 --> 00:53:43.780
and the mandolin lies within her vocal,

00:53:43.780 --> 00:53:46.660
her natural sort of vocal range.

00:53:46.660 --> 00:53:51.140
So I tried this out before

00:53:51.140 --> 00:53:54.180
we all gathered here with the panelists

00:53:54.180 --> 00:53:56.845
and it seemed a little
bit loud and distorted,

00:53:56.845 --> 00:53:59.960
but I'm gonna give it a try
'cause I will tell you that

00:53:59.960 --> 00:54:02.700
it also sounds kind of loud
and distorted from our home.

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:04.620
We'll have bluegrass playing

00:54:04.620 --> 00:54:06.760
and then Carmen will be singing along.

00:54:06.760 --> 00:54:09.263
So if you want to ask,

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:16.660
what I mean when I disbelieve the sense

00:54:16.660 --> 00:54:19.580
that only humans can
be engaged with music,

00:54:19.580 --> 00:54:21.450
I will show you that,

00:54:21.450 --> 00:54:25.233
what it looks like when
Carmen is engaged with music.

00:54:29.558 --> 00:54:34.558
(upbeat music)
(bird singing)

00:54:50.910 --> 00:54:53.050
So I hope that worked.

00:54:53.050 --> 00:54:53.883
Where's my...

00:54:57.011 --> 00:54:59.769
Don't worry, I won't make
you listen to that anymore.

00:54:59.769 --> 00:55:00.840
I hope you could get a sense of that.

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:03.090
So, oh wow, I do really have

00:55:05.310 --> 00:55:07.830
starling hair. (laughs)

00:55:07.830 --> 00:55:08.663
All right.

00:55:08.663 --> 00:55:12.590
What I love when I listen to
Carmen engaging with music

00:55:12.590 --> 00:55:16.520
is thinking about her interaction or

00:55:17.490 --> 00:55:21.080
Mozart's bird's interaction
with his family.

00:55:21.080 --> 00:55:24.270
And I actually believe
his starling to be male.

00:55:24.270 --> 00:55:26.620
So even though I said
that males and females

00:55:26.620 --> 00:55:28.683
are both really gifted mimics,

00:55:29.870 --> 00:55:31.860
just really sing the,

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:34.880
males have a little
bit of an edge on that.

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:37.390
It seems that females might be able to

00:55:39.110 --> 00:55:43.100
apprehend complexity in
sound and vocalizations

00:55:43.100 --> 00:55:44.850
better than male starlings can.

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:47.330
But males can mimic a little
bit better than females.

00:55:47.330 --> 00:55:50.980
And so because Mozart's
bird was such a gifted mimic

00:55:50.980 --> 00:55:54.390
and could mimic part of this concerto,

00:55:54.390 --> 00:55:56.180
I believe him to be a male.

00:55:56.180 --> 00:55:57.610
And plus it was easier for me to write

00:55:57.610 --> 00:56:00.280
to have a male starling
and a female starling.

00:56:00.280 --> 00:56:04.673
So I love thinking of Mozart
with his starling in his home.

00:56:05.950 --> 00:56:08.400
This is before he was married.

00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:11.840
Mozart with his sister, Nannerl,

00:56:11.840 --> 00:56:13.110
that's his mother, Anna Maria,

00:56:13.110 --> 00:56:15.014
in the back who has passed,

00:56:15.014 --> 00:56:18.487
and his father, Leopold.

00:56:18.487 --> 00:56:21.910
And this is getting long so I'm going to

00:56:25.978 --> 00:56:27.770
just say a couple more things.

00:56:27.770 --> 00:56:29.550
Anna Maria died

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:33.980
when Mozart was 21 in Paris.

00:56:33.980 --> 00:56:36.970
And Leopold died

00:56:38.500 --> 00:56:41.037
while Mozart had his starling

00:56:41.037 --> 00:56:45.535
and he lived in Vienna while
Leopold was living in Salzburg.

00:56:45.535 --> 00:56:48.130
Leopold died in 1787.

00:56:48.130 --> 00:56:49.300
He was 62 years old

00:56:49.300 --> 00:56:51.720
which was a pretty good age for the time.

00:56:51.720 --> 00:56:55.030
And at the time, Mozart
didn't attend the funeral.

00:56:55.030 --> 00:56:57.230
And much was made of this,

00:56:57.230 --> 00:56:59.280
and still is by biographers,

00:56:59.280 --> 00:57:02.710
that it shows that Leopold
was very kind of authoritarian

00:57:02.710 --> 00:57:06.340
and controlling and officious
with Mozart, which he was,

00:57:06.340 --> 00:57:08.160
but there's also, when
you read their letters,

00:57:08.160 --> 00:57:09.750
just so much love between them.

00:57:09.750 --> 00:57:12.060
At the time that Leopold died,

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:16.140
Mozart had young children in the house.

00:57:16.140 --> 00:57:19.640
Constanze was sick with a septic leg

00:57:19.640 --> 00:57:22.490
and their financial
situation was terrible.

00:57:22.490 --> 00:57:24.170
And by the standards of the day,

00:57:24.170 --> 00:57:25.960
a funeral was very expensive.

00:57:25.960 --> 00:57:28.890
So if Mozart went and
created a memorial service

00:57:28.890 --> 00:57:32.880
for his father in Salzburg,
remember 250 miles away,

00:57:32.880 --> 00:57:35.350
he would've had to lay out for
the expenses of the funeral

00:57:35.350 --> 00:57:36.730
and even hire mourners,

00:57:36.730 --> 00:57:38.570
which is something they did at the time.

00:57:38.570 --> 00:57:42.070
So it wasn't that Mozart didn't go

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:45.570
or didn't want to go to
Leopold's funeral, in my opinion.

00:57:45.570 --> 00:57:47.463
It's that he simply could not go.

00:57:48.610 --> 00:57:52.863
But two months after Leopold died,

00:57:53.850 --> 00:57:55.300
the starling died.

00:57:55.300 --> 00:57:58.230
And from his garden in Vienna,

00:57:58.230 --> 00:58:00.260
Mozart created a

00:58:03.930 --> 00:58:06.820
grand and formal funeral.

00:58:06.820 --> 00:58:09.050
So he buried the bird in the garden.

00:58:09.050 --> 00:58:10.630
He invited friends to wear

00:58:10.630 --> 00:58:14.010
formal mourning clothes and play music.

00:58:14.010 --> 00:58:16.483
And he even wrote an eulogy for the bird.

00:58:17.360 --> 00:58:20.470
And when people talk about this,

00:58:20.470 --> 00:58:24.300
this contrast in Mozart not
attending his father's funeral

00:58:24.300 --> 00:58:27.390
and then creating this
formal funeral for his bird,

00:58:27.390 --> 00:58:29.860
most people, most biographers
don't mention it again.

00:58:29.860 --> 00:58:32.920
But those that do pause
at two different theories.

00:58:32.920 --> 00:58:35.540
One is that it was simply a joke.

00:58:35.540 --> 00:58:38.610
(laughs) That it was over the top.

00:58:38.610 --> 00:58:41.160
Mozart was always a prankster

00:58:41.160 --> 00:58:43.377
and it would appeal to his sense of humor

00:58:43.377 --> 00:58:47.023
to create a formal funeral for a bird.

00:58:48.410 --> 00:58:50.710
And the other is that it was

00:58:50.710 --> 00:58:52.420
a way of grieving his father.

00:58:52.420 --> 00:58:54.680
That this is a little
bit of pop psychoanalysis

00:58:54.680 --> 00:58:56.140
that people are doing that sort of

00:58:56.140 --> 00:58:57.550
the vessel of the funeral

00:58:59.220 --> 00:59:01.610
in any form offered a
channel for the grief

00:59:01.610 --> 00:59:05.030
that he wasn't able to offer
in-person to his father.

00:59:05.030 --> 00:59:07.780
And I honestly believe that
both of those things make sense.

00:59:07.780 --> 00:59:10.330
Certainly a bird funeral would play

00:59:10.330 --> 00:59:12.843
to Mozart's wonderful sense of the absurd.

00:59:15.410 --> 00:59:19.020
And having a funeral could
be a container for his grief.

00:59:19.020 --> 00:59:23.130
But there's a third option
that biographers never mention,

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:25.460
but that anyone living with a starling

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:27.670
could not help but realize,

00:59:27.670 --> 00:59:31.620
and that is that it was just sincere.

00:59:31.620 --> 00:59:34.000
Mozart loved his starling.

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:38.550
Here's a bird that, like him,
was a good mimic. (laughs)

00:59:38.550 --> 00:59:43.550
Musical, mischievous,
intelligent and friendly.

00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:47.420
And he lost this bird during a time when

00:59:47.420 --> 00:59:49.800
he was in financial distress.

00:59:49.800 --> 00:59:51.470
He lost a child.

00:59:51.470 --> 00:59:53.500
He lost his father.

00:59:53.500 --> 00:59:56.130
He was working very hard
at the time his dad died.

00:59:56.130 --> 00:59:57.870
He was writing Don Giovanni.

00:59:57.870 --> 01:00:01.500
And here's this musical,
cheerful presence with him

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:03.320
during all of this.

01:00:03.320 --> 01:00:05.040
It was a sincere loss for him.

01:00:05.040 --> 01:00:07.590
And he wrote an eulogy and
I'm gonna read it to you

01:00:09.630 --> 01:00:11.220
because it's wonderful.

01:00:11.220 --> 01:00:12.520
This is Marsha Davenport's

01:00:15.990 --> 01:00:17.453
1932 translation.

01:00:19.190 --> 01:00:23.220
A little fool lies here whom I held dear.

01:00:23.220 --> 01:00:26.490
A starling in the prime of his brief time

01:00:26.490 --> 01:00:30.230
whose doom it was to
drain death's bitter pain.

01:00:30.230 --> 01:00:33.730
Thinking of this, my heart is riven apart.

01:00:33.730 --> 01:00:37.270
A reader shed a tear and you also hear

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:39.970
he was not naughty, quite,

01:00:39.970 --> 01:00:42.340
which is a line I love 'cause
that's what I think of Carmen.

01:00:42.340 --> 01:00:44.030
She's not naughty, quite.

01:00:44.030 --> 01:00:45.230
But gay and bright.

01:00:45.230 --> 01:00:48.460
And under all his brag, a foolish wag.

01:00:48.460 --> 01:00:51.120
This no one can gain say and I will lay

01:00:51.120 --> 01:00:54.830
that he is now on high and
from the sky praises me

01:00:54.830 --> 01:00:57.523
without pay in his friendly way.

01:00:58.420 --> 01:01:01.340
Yet unaware that death
has choked his breath

01:01:01.340 --> 01:01:05.233
and thoughtless of the one
whose rhyme is best well done.

01:01:12.590 --> 01:01:14.240
I visited Mozart's grave.

01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:16.820
No one knows exactly where he's buried but

01:01:18.690 --> 01:01:19.523
because it's getting late,

01:01:19.523 --> 01:01:20.980
I think I'm just gonna kind of move on.

01:01:20.980 --> 01:01:22.960
This is his grave in Vienna.

01:01:22.960 --> 01:01:26.580
It's at this beautiful tangled ancient

01:01:26.580 --> 01:01:30.480
kind of haunted cemetery outside of town.

01:01:30.480 --> 01:01:33.190
And there used to be a
really big memorial there

01:01:33.190 --> 01:01:36.330
out of granite and sort of
the muse in contemplation.

01:01:36.330 --> 01:01:38.870
And they took that
memorial in the late 1800s

01:01:38.870 --> 01:01:43.870
and they moved it to the new
Vienna Municipal City Cemetery.

01:01:44.680 --> 01:01:46.040
Other side of town.

01:01:46.040 --> 01:01:48.100
And so all kinds of Mozart pilgrims go

01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:49.700
and they think that big memorial

01:01:49.700 --> 01:01:51.810
that it was sort of created to be this

01:01:51.810 --> 01:01:54.160
garden of dead musicians,

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:57.030
Schumann is buried there,
Salieri, Beethoven,

01:01:57.030 --> 01:02:01.130
and then they added this
memorial statue of Mozart.

01:02:01.130 --> 01:02:02.710
You have to read the
fine print to find out

01:02:02.710 --> 01:02:04.640
that Mozart's actually there.

01:02:04.640 --> 01:02:05.473
Not there.

01:02:05.473 --> 01:02:08.170
But in the actual graveyard where he is,

01:02:08.170 --> 01:02:09.570
he was buried in a common grave,

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:11.250
which was the practice at the time.

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:14.210
And so they didn't put grave
markers in the exact place.

01:02:14.210 --> 01:02:16.690
And so this is in the approximate place.

01:02:16.690 --> 01:02:18.420
And when I was there,

01:02:18.420 --> 01:02:21.350
you can't really tell that jungley tangle,

01:02:21.350 --> 01:02:24.020
the sense of boldness and hauntedness

01:02:24.020 --> 01:02:26.140
that enchants this cemetery.

01:02:26.140 --> 01:02:27.990
But one of the things
that really struck me was

01:02:27.990 --> 01:02:30.710
I kind of leaned down and
looked up at this cherub

01:02:30.710 --> 01:02:32.660
and I expected him to be

01:02:34.260 --> 01:02:36.500
contemplating some sort of,

01:02:36.500 --> 01:02:38.160
the music of the spheres

01:02:38.160 --> 01:02:40.810
and a sense of the spirit of Mozart.

01:02:40.810 --> 01:02:43.190
But all I really got was this look of,

01:02:43.190 --> 01:02:48.190
oh my gosh, I'm here forever
and he's not even buried here.

01:02:49.210 --> 01:02:50.503
Just made me laugh.

01:02:52.690 --> 01:02:55.540
There are now 200 million

01:02:55.540 --> 01:02:58.790
starlings in North America.

01:02:58.790 --> 01:03:01.110
And ecologically they are

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:04.890
somewhat disastrous in certain ways.

01:03:04.890 --> 01:03:08.960
But individually they
have these bright minds,

01:03:08.960 --> 01:03:11.180
these shining feathers,

01:03:11.180 --> 01:03:15.490
this lovely unique consciousness
that is so instructive.

01:03:15.490 --> 01:03:18.110
I think it makes us aware
when we walk in the world

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:22.020
that we are so deeply interconnected to

01:03:24.260 --> 01:03:27.310
all of the kindred

01:03:27.310 --> 01:03:30.113
of all of the more than human world.

01:03:35.080 --> 01:03:37.460
And so I'm gonna just
finish with one little

01:03:40.610 --> 01:03:42.320
sentence from the book.

01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:45.270
And I'll let us keep
watching this murmuration.

01:03:45.270 --> 01:03:47.630
We can talk about how
murmurations work in the Q&amp;A

01:03:47.630 --> 01:03:48.580
if you all want to.

01:03:51.010 --> 01:03:53.170
So it is in reciprocity

01:03:53.170 --> 01:03:56.620
to this idea of wonder and complexity

01:03:56.620 --> 01:03:59.920
that we discover what draws
us, and along with it,

01:03:59.920 --> 01:04:02.860
our originality, our creativity,

01:04:02.860 --> 01:04:07.040
our soulness, our gladness, our art.

01:04:07.040 --> 01:04:10.930
Mozart found inspiration in
the presence of a common bird.

01:04:10.930 --> 01:04:14.710
For us too, the song of
the world so often rises

01:04:14.710 --> 01:04:18.193
in places we had not thought to look.

01:04:20.890 --> 01:04:24.830
So I'm gonna end with Mozart
and the starling there

01:04:24.830 --> 01:04:25.663
for the moment.

01:04:25.663 --> 01:04:27.750
I'll try to invite Carmen back.

01:04:27.750 --> 01:04:29.430
I've got her favorite applesauce.

01:04:29.430 --> 01:04:31.020
We'll try to draw her over.

01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:36.020
But I'm going to just briefly
say, and I apologize for this,

01:04:36.820 --> 01:04:39.760
but I have a book coming out next week

01:04:39.760 --> 01:04:42.930
and I am basically under
contractual obligation

01:04:42.930 --> 01:04:45.210
to mention it whenever I can.

01:04:45.210 --> 01:04:47.660
My publicist wanted something
of me this afternoon

01:04:47.660 --> 01:04:49.780
and I said, I can't deal with that.

01:04:49.780 --> 01:04:51.520
I'm preparing for this lecture.

01:04:51.520 --> 01:04:54.410
And she said, well, make
sure you mention your book.

01:04:54.410 --> 01:04:56.060
And I'm slightly frightened of her.

01:04:56.060 --> 01:04:57.520
So I'm mentioning my book.

01:04:57.520 --> 01:04:58.830
Rooted: Life at the Crossroads

01:04:58.830 --> 01:05:01.803
of Science, Nature, and
Spirit is available next week.

01:05:02.670 --> 01:05:04.070
I hope that you'll stay in touch.

01:05:04.070 --> 01:05:05.313
That's my personal,

01:05:06.520 --> 01:05:09.274
oh my personal, I thought I
had my email address on there.

01:05:09.274 --> 01:05:12.520
It's just lyandahaupt@gmail.com.

01:05:12.520 --> 01:05:14.580
Please feel free to contact me

01:05:14.580 --> 01:05:19.560
and follow along on any of the socials.

01:05:19.560 --> 01:05:21.620
All right, I'd be
delighted to stay in touch.

01:05:21.620 --> 01:05:24.293
So I'm gonna pop off
the stop share screen.

01:05:27.016 --> 01:05:28.210
(mumbling)

01:05:28.210 --> 01:05:29.367
- Great, thank you very much.

01:05:29.367 --> 01:05:31.170
What a lovely talk.
- And say that,

01:05:31.170 --> 01:05:33.440
I just want to say thank you.

01:05:33.440 --> 01:05:36.840
I know this is a really platform.

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:40.600
I'm a really conversational speaker.

01:05:40.600 --> 01:05:42.580
- Right now is the fun part,

01:05:42.580 --> 01:05:45.193
'cause from here on in
it's gonna be real easy.

01:05:46.132 --> 01:05:48.895
The vast majority of
your videos and sounds

01:05:48.895 --> 01:05:50.730
were perfectly fine.

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:52.490
- Okay, good.
- Especially some of the

01:05:52.490 --> 01:05:54.980
videos that were challenging before.

01:05:54.980 --> 01:05:58.050
So yeah, I just want
to reflect that I think

01:05:58.050 --> 01:06:00.540
anyone who has experienced

01:06:02.124 --> 01:06:05.400
a bird as a pet or a domesticated bird

01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:08.250
understands the kind of way that

01:06:09.590 --> 01:06:13.894
it connects people to the
intelligence of birds,

01:06:13.894 --> 01:06:17.500
the personality of birds in a unique way.

01:06:17.500 --> 01:06:21.200
And so I think you're exactly right.

01:06:21.200 --> 01:06:25.863
The avian intelligences,
plural, is a big part.

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:30.150
And I don't think anybody
who also had a pet bird

01:06:30.150 --> 01:06:35.150
could doubt that Mozart
was sincere and not joking

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:40.660
in his celebration of the
life of this pet starling.

01:06:40.660 --> 01:06:41.493
That was good.

01:06:42.740 --> 01:06:45.060
So we have lots of comments in the,

01:06:45.060 --> 01:06:47.933
and questions in the feed.
- Great.

01:06:48.950 --> 01:06:51.200
- And I just remind, for those
of you who raise your hand,

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:53.710
if you can put your questions in the chat

01:06:53.710 --> 01:06:55.010
then that will help us.

01:06:55.010 --> 01:06:56.570
And I think I'll just start

01:06:56.570 --> 01:06:58.770
with some of the questions
I've been reviewing it.

01:06:58.770 --> 01:07:02.200
And Lyanda, if you want to field

01:07:02.200 --> 01:07:04.620
or if you need any, if you have any,

01:07:04.620 --> 01:07:06.180
or want to have a conversation
about any of these,

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:07.050
I'd be glad to help out.

01:07:07.050 --> 01:07:10.960
So the first comes from Jane
McCall-Politi, who asked,

01:07:10.960 --> 01:07:14.271
do starlings pass on
their linguistic abilities

01:07:14.271 --> 01:07:16.533
to their offspring, to their children?

01:07:17.590 --> 01:07:20.670
- Great, well, pass on
is an interesting phrase.

01:07:20.670 --> 01:07:25.640
So it's unlikely that they
pass it on genetically,

01:07:25.640 --> 01:07:27.900
but they do teach their offspring

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:30.310
some of their unique phrases.

01:07:30.310 --> 01:07:33.370
So starlings have a lot
of complex vocalizations

01:07:33.370 --> 01:07:35.970
that people hearing
them think that they're,

01:07:35.970 --> 01:07:37.580
it's just wild craziness,

01:07:37.580 --> 01:07:38.500
but when you study them

01:07:38.500 --> 01:07:40.470
you find that there's an order to it.

01:07:40.470 --> 01:07:43.600
But part of that order is
in every starling song,

01:07:43.600 --> 01:07:46.110
it starts with a whistle
and then moves on to,

01:07:46.110 --> 01:07:49.100
there's this little, some long whistles

01:07:49.100 --> 01:07:50.980
and a crescendo and some rattles.

01:07:50.980 --> 01:07:54.350
There's a section for personal repertoire.

01:07:54.350 --> 01:07:57.750
So they will throw in
sounds that they favor,

01:07:57.750 --> 01:08:00.140
that they've learned, that
they've mimicked themselves,

01:08:00.140 --> 01:08:02.800
and it varies from
individual to individual.

01:08:02.800 --> 01:08:05.160
But we do find that in family groups,

01:08:05.160 --> 01:08:07.340
males and female partners, mates,

01:08:07.340 --> 01:08:12.330
will start picking up and
mimicking back to one another

01:08:12.330 --> 01:08:15.490
each individual's own mimic sounds.

01:08:15.490 --> 01:08:18.250
And then the young will
also pick up on the

01:08:19.230 --> 01:08:22.850
unique individual
repertoires of the parents.

01:08:22.850 --> 01:08:25.110
That said, the young will go on

01:08:25.110 --> 01:08:26.680
and create their own repertoire

01:08:26.680 --> 01:08:29.440
and choose their own
mimic sounds to add in.

01:08:29.440 --> 01:08:32.970
So there's a lot of sharing and learning

01:08:32.970 --> 01:08:36.290
but it doesn't seem to
be genetically passed on.

01:08:36.290 --> 01:08:37.540
- Yeah, yeah, great.

01:08:37.540 --> 01:08:40.990
And so a question from Susan Honi is,

01:08:40.990 --> 01:08:44.883
how do starlings compare
to the lyrebirds as mimics?

01:08:45.874 --> 01:08:49.260
- Oh, nothing compares to
the lyrebirds as mimics.

01:08:51.530 --> 01:08:54.320
We've all seen the David
Attenborough footage

01:08:54.320 --> 01:08:58.860
of the lyrebird doing
the rewind of the camera.

01:08:58.860 --> 01:09:02.711
And I personally feel that starlings,

01:09:02.711 --> 01:09:04.930
although they are so gifted

01:09:04.930 --> 01:09:08.800
and they can pick up so much
and they can learn songs

01:09:08.800 --> 01:09:11.160
and it's incredibly impressive,

01:09:11.160 --> 01:09:14.610
there's always a whistly birdiness to it.

01:09:14.610 --> 01:09:16.090
And the things that

01:09:17.680 --> 01:09:19.980
Carmen and other starlings
mimic that sound,

01:09:19.980 --> 01:09:22.450
when I say it's pitch perfect
and we can't tell them apart,

01:09:22.450 --> 01:09:25.430
that's something like
the beep of a microwave.

01:09:25.430 --> 01:09:28.530
So that is a very kind
of birdish sound anyway.

01:09:28.530 --> 01:09:31.000
But otherwise there's a
whistly birdiness to it,

01:09:31.000 --> 01:09:35.929
that in the lyrebird, somehow
they're on another plane.

01:09:35.929 --> 01:09:39.347
So humans have larynxes,
birds have syrinxes.

01:09:39.347 --> 01:09:42.290
And so there's a lot of
variation in the vocal cords,

01:09:42.290 --> 01:09:43.520
in the vocal apparatus,

01:09:43.520 --> 01:09:47.890
that allow different species
to have different capacities.

01:09:47.890 --> 01:09:52.890
And the lyrebird is
like the great. (laughs)

01:09:54.207 --> 01:09:57.260
Feel free to--
- Really too, I mean,

01:09:57.260 --> 01:09:59.840
the closest relatives to the starlings,

01:09:59.840 --> 01:10:01.960
which are about 120 species total,

01:10:01.960 --> 01:10:05.240
are the new world mockingbirds
and their relatives.

01:10:05.240 --> 01:10:07.830
So the capacity for mimicry evolutionarily

01:10:07.830 --> 01:10:10.010
goes quite far back in this history.

01:10:10.010 --> 01:10:11.370
But not all starlings do it.

01:10:11.370 --> 01:10:12.860
And the other way to
think about it is that

01:10:12.860 --> 01:10:15.683
starlings have more of
their own stuff to say.

01:10:16.570 --> 01:10:20.090
So mimicry is a smaller
part of their repertoire

01:10:20.090 --> 01:10:23.130
'cause they have starling stuff to do.

01:10:23.130 --> 01:10:25.090
And that's less true of lyrebird,

01:10:25.090 --> 01:10:28.290
who is mostly composing its repertoire

01:10:28.290 --> 01:10:30.400
from other species' songs.

01:10:30.400 --> 01:10:31.610
- Yeah, that's really interesting.

01:10:31.610 --> 01:10:34.150
Yeah, starlings have a really
high plasticity of behavior

01:10:34.150 --> 01:10:36.690
so they have a lot going on, yeah.

01:10:36.690 --> 01:10:39.400
- So a question from Bruce MacIntyre.

01:10:39.400 --> 01:10:41.083
Do birds have perfect pitch?

01:10:44.720 --> 01:10:46.389
What do you think?

01:10:46.389 --> 01:10:47.950
- I don't know, I don't know.

01:10:47.950 --> 01:10:49.813
I mean, starlings,

01:10:50.990 --> 01:10:52.500
do you know the answer to that?

01:10:52.500 --> 01:10:53.730
- Well, I would say,

01:10:53.730 --> 01:10:55.580
I don't think they have names for pitches

01:10:55.580 --> 01:10:58.640
so they're not gonna say,
oh, that's a C sharp.

01:10:58.640 --> 01:11:03.270
But obviously in order to
learn from conspecifics,

01:11:03.270 --> 01:11:07.060
they do have to match on on frequency.

01:11:07.060 --> 01:11:08.970
And what's also interesting
of course is that

01:11:08.970 --> 01:11:12.780
they have the individual
motivation to do, perhaps innovate.

01:11:12.780 --> 01:11:14.470
So they don't do it slavishly,

01:11:14.470 --> 01:11:18.410
but they clearly have
that capacity, I think.

01:11:18.410 --> 01:11:20.650
- Right, and when you
hear young birds learning,

01:11:20.650 --> 01:11:21.900
especially males in the fall,

01:11:21.900 --> 01:11:25.968
sometimes they'll already
start learning their song,

01:11:25.968 --> 01:11:28.320
their adult song.

01:11:28.320 --> 01:11:29.950
And they're very imperfect.

01:11:29.950 --> 01:11:32.310
So we can hear them
working to get it right.

01:11:32.310 --> 01:11:35.580
And I hear that with Carmen
when she is learning to mimic.

01:11:35.580 --> 01:11:37.740
Her current project is the Bewick's wren

01:11:37.740 --> 01:11:41.667
who's singing outside in the
camellia outside of her window.

01:11:41.667 --> 01:11:43.440
And she practices.

01:11:43.440 --> 01:11:44.750
She gets it wrong and wrong

01:11:44.750 --> 01:11:48.080
and then she eventually gets it right.

01:11:48.080 --> 01:11:49.954
- That's social learning.

01:11:49.954 --> 01:11:52.430
One of my advisors, Bob Payne,

01:11:52.430 --> 01:11:56.390
published a paper that a local house finch

01:11:56.390 --> 01:11:59.310
had learned from a canary that

01:11:59.310 --> 01:12:02.130
one of the neighbors put out on her porch

01:12:02.130 --> 01:12:03.660
so that the bird could sing outside.

01:12:03.660 --> 01:12:06.380
So it goes even from
domestication into the wild,

01:12:06.380 --> 01:12:08.420
not just the other way.

01:12:08.420 --> 01:12:10.820
- So fun.
- A question from Susan.

01:12:10.820 --> 01:12:14.363
Is Carmen a female and does
she lay unfertilized eggs?

01:12:15.350 --> 01:12:16.610
- Okay, Carmen is a female.

01:12:16.610 --> 01:12:18.270
And it's really hard to tell,

01:12:18.270 --> 01:12:20.270
unless you've done a lot
of starling watching,

01:12:20.270 --> 01:12:22.980
there's not a lot of
dimorphism between the feathers

01:12:22.980 --> 01:12:24.410
of the males and females.

01:12:24.410 --> 01:12:25.810
But in the breeding season especially,

01:12:25.810 --> 01:12:29.380
the males have these sort of
punky feathers on their wings

01:12:29.380 --> 01:12:33.874
and their neck feathers
are extra plumey and,

01:12:33.874 --> 01:12:34.830
uh-oh, there's a cat on the loose.

01:12:34.830 --> 01:12:37.230
I finally remembered
to put the kitten away.

01:12:37.230 --> 01:12:39.203
This cat is very fat though.

01:12:40.672 --> 01:12:41.670
Give me one second.

01:12:41.670 --> 01:12:43.605
I'm just gonna lock this fat cat up.

01:12:43.605 --> 01:12:44.555
Come here, Delilah.

01:12:48.786 --> 01:12:51.036
(laughing)

01:12:52.266 --> 01:12:54.910
- That is just, that has
been such a fascinating talk.

01:12:54.910 --> 01:12:58.680
Oh my God, so many questions, yeah.

01:12:58.680 --> 01:13:00.050
So Rick, do--

01:13:00.050 --> 01:13:02.270
- We don't want any tragedies here.

01:13:02.270 --> 01:13:03.103
- No.

01:13:04.120 --> 01:13:08.310
- Lyanda, Rick, I cannot
resist chiming in here.

01:13:08.310 --> 01:13:11.943
So does Carmen have her personal song?

01:13:14.210 --> 01:13:15.870
- Yeah, so, okay.

01:13:15.870 --> 01:13:17.890
So what Carmen does is,

01:13:17.890 --> 01:13:19.790
and this is really interesting because

01:13:21.170 --> 01:13:23.040
a lot of passerine birds have

01:13:23.040 --> 01:13:25.669
vocalizations that are innate.

01:13:25.669 --> 01:13:28.860
If you get them when they're
young like I did Carmen

01:13:28.860 --> 01:13:31.550
and she wasn't really
exposed to Carmen's songs,

01:13:31.550 --> 01:13:34.970
and yet she sings the full range of

01:13:36.200 --> 01:13:37.970
the typical female song.

01:13:37.970 --> 01:13:39.548
So it starts with a whistle

01:13:39.548 --> 01:13:42.620
and then her own repertoire of sounds.

01:13:42.620 --> 01:13:43.453
And then

01:13:45.270 --> 01:13:47.100
another kind of rattly sound

01:13:47.100 --> 01:13:49.270
and this crescendo whistle at the end.

01:13:49.270 --> 01:13:52.050
And males add in this rattling song thing.

01:13:52.050 --> 01:13:56.290
So it goes, there's just a formula

01:13:56.290 --> 01:13:58.320
for starling songs and she sings it.

01:13:58.320 --> 01:14:02.820
But she definitely has her
own repertoire of mimic sounds

01:14:02.820 --> 01:14:04.070
and she will integrate that

01:14:04.070 --> 01:14:07.890
into the typical starling pattern.

01:14:07.890 --> 01:14:09.010
It's fascinating.

01:14:09.010 --> 01:14:11.590
I hope that, is that what your question?

01:14:11.590 --> 01:14:13.010
- Yeah, that was my question.

01:14:13.010 --> 01:14:16.370
And I guess it would be great, Rick.

01:14:16.370 --> 01:14:21.247
And does the signature
song change with age?

01:14:21.247 --> 01:14:23.740
- Well, as Lyanda pointed out,

01:14:23.740 --> 01:14:27.110
most of the birds that learn their songs

01:14:29.900 --> 01:14:32.390
learn or basically hit the target

01:14:32.390 --> 01:14:33.950
within the end of the first year,

01:14:33.950 --> 01:14:35.910
the first spring or the second year.

01:14:35.910 --> 01:14:37.320
And then stay,

01:14:37.320 --> 01:14:39.930
the single song or the
entire repertoire unchanged.

01:14:39.930 --> 01:14:42.660
There are, however, some birds
that are much more open-ended

01:14:42.660 --> 01:14:44.980
and mimicry is a great
way to get open-ended.

01:14:44.980 --> 01:14:47.450
That's probably why mimicry evolved at all

01:14:47.450 --> 01:14:50.877
because there was either
social choice or made choice

01:14:50.877 --> 01:14:53.580
for a very open or extreme repertoire.

01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:55.450
And if you keep learning other songs,

01:14:55.450 --> 01:14:57.490
your repertoire keeps growing.

01:14:57.490 --> 01:15:00.693
It's more innovative and novel.

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:04.485
But another bird that learns their song

01:15:04.485 --> 01:15:06.940
continuously throughout life is a canary,

01:15:06.940 --> 01:15:09.100
which is the wild species that

01:15:09.100 --> 01:15:10.590
the domestic canary came from.

01:15:10.590 --> 01:15:14.350
So canaries learn their song renewed.

01:15:14.350 --> 01:15:16.520
Every spring, they have, the wild ones,

01:15:16.520 --> 01:15:21.520
have a new rush of song
innovation and learning.

01:15:23.404 --> 01:15:25.490
Made a note back in the '90s.

01:15:25.490 --> 01:15:27.020
I found out that that
was actually associated

01:15:27.020 --> 01:15:30.010
with the origin of new
neurons in the brain

01:15:30.010 --> 01:15:31.450
and on control areas,

01:15:31.450 --> 01:15:34.730
which was part of the discovery

01:15:34.730 --> 01:15:38.040
that bird brains can grow new neurons,

01:15:38.040 --> 01:15:39.827
which prior to that was not understood.

01:15:39.827 --> 01:15:42.760
But that fundamental biological fact

01:15:42.760 --> 01:15:45.140
which is now underlies
treatment for stroke

01:15:45.140 --> 01:15:47.820
and neurodegenerative disorders in humans

01:15:47.820 --> 01:15:49.485
was discovered in bird song

01:15:49.485 --> 01:15:52.183
with exactly this question in mind.

01:15:52.183 --> 01:15:56.290
- And Lyanda, just a quick
final comment slash question.

01:15:56.290 --> 01:15:57.670
It might be crazy.

01:15:57.670 --> 01:16:02.033
But Lyanda, do you hear
emotion when she improvises?

01:16:03.123 --> 01:16:04.458
- Oh.

01:16:04.458 --> 01:16:07.037
- I know this is probably
not a fair question.

01:16:07.037 --> 01:16:09.677
And it's deeply possible that you--

01:16:11.363 --> 01:16:13.010
- I don't know about emotion.

01:16:13.010 --> 01:16:16.763
I mean, I think that she is definitely,

01:16:18.990 --> 01:16:21.580
she gets more excited when she hears

01:16:21.580 --> 01:16:24.380
certain music compared to other music.

01:16:24.380 --> 01:16:27.746
And I personally, I have
to say, I'm not against,

01:16:27.746 --> 01:16:29.880
in science, there's this whole,

01:16:29.880 --> 01:16:32.010
oh my gosh, we don't
want to anthropomorphize.

01:16:32.010 --> 01:16:36.210
And I actually think along 160 years ago,

01:16:36.210 --> 01:16:40.700
Darwin himself said that he didn't,

01:16:40.700 --> 01:16:45.330
in positing natural selection
as a mechanism for evolution,

01:16:45.330 --> 01:16:46.720
he didn't see any difference

01:16:46.720 --> 01:16:51.527
in positing condition of morphological

01:16:52.500 --> 01:16:55.500
validity and consciousness both.

01:16:55.500 --> 01:16:56.730
He didn't think it made any sense

01:16:56.730 --> 01:16:58.730
that there was a
morphological continuation

01:16:58.730 --> 01:17:01.100
but consciousness somehow
stopped with humans.

01:17:01.100 --> 01:17:02.750
And he readily called animals

01:17:02.750 --> 01:17:05.000
peaceable, calm, happy, whatever.

01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:08.630
So, and I think that we've
really, really restricted

01:17:08.630 --> 01:17:10.960
the ways that we understand
different intelligences

01:17:10.960 --> 01:17:14.510
by not allowing ourselves
to just acknowledge that

01:17:14.510 --> 01:17:17.210
sometimes we recognize
when an animal is in pain

01:17:17.210 --> 01:17:20.370
or is in distress or agitated.

01:17:20.370 --> 01:17:22.270
At the same time, we don't
want to over-attribute.

01:17:22.270 --> 01:17:25.310
So when you say emotions,
I paused for a minute,

01:17:25.310 --> 01:17:28.073
but I do think that there
are times when she's engaged,

01:17:28.073 --> 01:17:32.670
when she's satisfied,
when she's not agitated.

01:17:32.670 --> 01:17:35.520
And a lot of that has to do
with what's going on aurally.

01:17:36.850 --> 01:17:39.980
- Lyanda, if I may, we
have a great question

01:17:39.980 --> 01:17:41.900
from Professor Brown,

01:17:41.900 --> 01:17:44.720
who's President of the
Mozart Society of America.

01:17:44.720 --> 01:17:48.480
He says, the L at the end of starl,

01:17:48.480 --> 01:17:50.310
as it was called in that initial note

01:17:50.310 --> 01:17:52.500
on the purchase and the ledger

01:17:52.500 --> 01:17:54.570
makes it a diminutive in the German.

01:17:54.570 --> 01:17:56.830
Do you imagine that it was a baby,

01:17:56.830 --> 01:18:00.263
a chick starling, when
Mozart purchased Star?

01:18:03.390 --> 01:18:05.180
- I mean, I can look into it further,

01:18:05.180 --> 01:18:07.530
but as I understand it, that was a,

01:18:11.620 --> 01:18:16.369
that was one of the German
common names for starling.

01:18:16.369 --> 01:18:17.550
(speaking in foreign language)

01:18:17.550 --> 01:18:20.330
And it meant little star because,

01:18:20.330 --> 01:18:22.240
and it's one of the reasons
that we call starlings

01:18:22.240 --> 01:18:23.750
starlings in their common name.

01:18:23.750 --> 01:18:27.620
And they fly, they have pointed wings,

01:18:27.620 --> 01:18:28.810
pointed tails, pointed noses,

01:18:28.810 --> 01:18:31.760
and they look like little
stars when they're in flight.

01:18:31.760 --> 01:18:34.290
And that's one theory of where
the common name came from.

01:18:34.290 --> 01:18:37.300
And so it might've just been little star

01:18:37.300 --> 01:18:38.950
rather than baby starling.

01:18:38.950 --> 01:18:42.080
And Mozart was a decent naturalist.

01:18:42.080 --> 01:18:43.160
He knew something about

01:18:43.160 --> 01:18:45.200
or he was interested in the natural world,

01:18:45.200 --> 01:18:48.910
and he certainly knew the common
species name for the bird.

01:18:48.910 --> 01:18:52.190
And so later biographers reading
that note would think that,

01:18:52.190 --> 01:18:55.810
thought that he named the
bird Star based on that note.

01:18:55.810 --> 01:18:57.570
But he was really just labeling it

01:18:57.570 --> 01:18:58.620
according to the common name.

01:18:58.620 --> 01:19:02.990
So to answer the question though,

01:19:02.990 --> 01:19:04.570
it's unlikely that it was a baby

01:19:04.570 --> 01:19:07.500
because one of the
things that caused Mozart

01:19:07.500 --> 01:19:11.610
or urged Mozart to purchase
this particular bird

01:19:11.610 --> 01:19:14.040
was that it was singing the

01:19:14.040 --> 01:19:15.660
motif from the concerto.

01:19:15.660 --> 01:19:18.660
And birds, even gifted
mimics like starlings,

01:19:18.660 --> 01:19:21.440
are not able to mimic
that something complex

01:19:21.440 --> 01:19:23.060
until they're several years old.

01:19:23.060 --> 01:19:25.880
And I, or rather, several months old.

01:19:25.880 --> 01:19:28.853
And for many reasons,
given the time of year,

01:19:29.770 --> 01:19:31.150
it was May.

01:19:31.150 --> 01:19:35.090
And so starlings are young in May,

01:19:35.090 --> 01:19:36.940
are born in May even in

01:19:39.060 --> 01:19:41.550
now Austria where Mozart was.

01:19:41.550 --> 01:19:43.690
And so it could not have been a baby.

01:19:43.690 --> 01:19:45.770
It would have had to be
about a year old at that time

01:19:45.770 --> 01:19:49.910
to be singing that level of complexity.

01:19:49.910 --> 01:19:52.780
So my conjecture is that
it's a year old bird.

01:19:52.780 --> 01:19:54.660
And he died three years later.

01:19:54.660 --> 01:19:56.563
So at about four years of age.

01:19:57.760 --> 01:19:58.643
Great question.

01:19:59.530 --> 01:20:00.770
- Indeed.

01:20:00.770 --> 01:20:04.560
In August 1784, Emperor
Joseph the II decreed

01:20:04.560 --> 01:20:08.060
new burial regulations in
Vienna for sanitation reasons,

01:20:08.060 --> 01:20:11.280
which later led to Mozart's
quote unquote anonymous

01:20:11.280 --> 01:20:13.510
shroud burial in 1791.

01:20:13.510 --> 01:20:15.070
By burying his dear Star,

01:20:15.070 --> 01:20:18.490
the composer was probably breaking a law.

01:20:18.490 --> 01:20:22.300
And that's thanks to Bruce
MacIntyre, great contribution.

01:20:22.300 --> 01:20:23.133
- Yeah.

01:20:24.520 --> 01:20:26.550
It's true, people feel that Mozart was,

01:20:26.550 --> 01:20:29.670
there's this myth that Mozart
was buried in a pauper's grave

01:20:29.670 --> 01:20:31.770
and no one attended his funeral.

01:20:31.770 --> 01:20:33.420
And it's seen as this
view that he was sort of

01:20:33.420 --> 01:20:36.370
spat upon by the city to
which he gave so much.

01:20:36.370 --> 01:20:40.120
But as the commenter says, it's just,

01:20:40.120 --> 01:20:42.660
it was new regulations by Joseph the II

01:20:42.660 --> 01:20:46.360
and for sanitation and for frugality.

01:20:46.360 --> 01:20:48.690
There was so much excess in the funeral

01:20:49.970 --> 01:20:51.260
processions before that.

01:20:51.260 --> 01:20:53.852
And these were changes
that Mozart agreed with.

01:20:53.852 --> 01:20:56.260
So yeah, he was buried in a grave

01:20:56.260 --> 01:20:57.540
with about 10 other people.

01:20:57.540 --> 01:20:59.690
The shroud, through some lye on top.

01:20:59.690 --> 01:21:02.250
And then in 10 years' time,

01:21:02.250 --> 01:21:04.473
they would reuse that
plot for other people.

01:21:05.970 --> 01:21:10.330
- We had a participant ask
about the participatory mode,

01:21:10.330 --> 01:21:11.930
I think you were mentioning.

01:21:11.930 --> 01:21:14.610
Do you remember that part of your talk?

01:21:14.610 --> 01:21:16.693
And could you go over that again, please?

01:21:17.870 --> 01:21:21.030
- Okay, so what I was
really saying is that

01:21:22.740 --> 01:21:26.750
what I initially thought in
terms of Carmen's mimicry

01:21:26.750 --> 01:21:28.870
was participation in the household.

01:21:28.870 --> 01:21:31.130
That she was just picking
up environmental sounds.

01:21:31.130 --> 01:21:32.260
And I'm gonna be,

01:21:32.260 --> 01:21:34.520
I'm gonna tell you,

01:21:34.520 --> 01:21:36.400
this is gonna reflect poorly upon me

01:21:36.400 --> 01:21:39.400
but the first environmental
sound that she learned,

01:21:39.400 --> 01:21:42.160
she could say, hi and
hi, honey and come here.

01:21:42.160 --> 01:21:44.727
But I heard her making the
sound that went (creaking).

01:21:46.091 --> 01:21:48.280
And I thought, what is that sound?

01:21:48.280 --> 01:21:50.000
And I finally figured out one day

01:21:50.000 --> 01:21:53.516
that it's the vacuum on the wine cork.

01:21:53.516 --> 01:21:54.930
You know how you have those

01:21:54.930 --> 01:21:56.660
that vacuums out the extra air?

01:21:56.660 --> 01:22:00.270
So her first environmental sound was

01:22:00.270 --> 01:22:03.840
associated with me hitting
the bottle. (laughs)

01:22:03.840 --> 01:22:06.550
Not really hitting the bottle,
just sipping the Chardonnay.

01:22:06.550 --> 01:22:10.220
But anyway, I kind of thought
that, for a long time,

01:22:10.220 --> 01:22:12.530
that she was just
learning household sounds.

01:22:12.530 --> 01:22:15.830
But what turned out to be
the actual case was that

01:22:15.830 --> 01:22:19.270
although she was learning
to mimic household sounds,

01:22:19.270 --> 01:22:21.980
she wasn't just doing them along with us.

01:22:21.980 --> 01:22:25.520
She was doing them in anticipation

01:22:25.520 --> 01:22:28.900
of the sound that she knew
was going to happen next.

01:22:28.900 --> 01:22:32.567
And that is the fascination of the,

01:22:36.940 --> 01:22:39.940
just the complexity of
the mindscape of this bird

01:22:39.940 --> 01:22:41.610
that she introduced to me.

01:22:41.610 --> 01:22:45.510
I mean, when I brought
Carmen into the home

01:22:45.510 --> 01:22:47.730
as part of research
for creating this book,

01:22:47.730 --> 01:22:50.120
I kind of thought I knew
what was gonna happen.

01:22:50.120 --> 01:22:53.630
She was gonna mimic a lot and
that was gonna be interesting.

01:22:53.630 --> 01:22:58.200
She might learn the Mozart
motif and that would be great.

01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:00.990
But instead she just
really upended my sense

01:23:00.990 --> 01:23:04.560
of the possibilities of the
infinity of intelligences

01:23:04.560 --> 01:23:09.560
that surround us in
the beyond human world.

01:23:09.560 --> 01:23:13.420
So that idea of, and
this is little understood

01:23:13.420 --> 01:23:16.040
in the literature, little
researched in the literature,

01:23:16.040 --> 01:23:18.570
because it's something
that you can't figure out

01:23:18.570 --> 01:23:20.320
just hanging out with a bird in a lab.

01:23:20.320 --> 01:23:22.100
But it just takes months of sort of

01:23:22.100 --> 01:23:23.970
living day in and day out

01:23:23.970 --> 01:23:25.890
to finally figure out the pattern that

01:23:25.890 --> 01:23:27.730
she's not just singing along

01:23:27.730 --> 01:23:29.300
or not just singing your favorite songs.

01:23:29.300 --> 01:23:32.850
She's listening to you
open the microwave door

01:23:32.850 --> 01:23:35.770
and then before the beeps
come, she's making the beeps.

01:23:35.770 --> 01:23:38.240
She's anticipating
what's gonna happen next.

01:23:38.240 --> 01:23:41.380
She's just there waiting to say, I'm here.

01:23:41.380 --> 01:23:43.480
I know what's gonna happen and here it is.

01:23:45.291 --> 01:23:46.124
It's wonderful.

01:23:50.980 --> 01:23:51.950
- One of the last questions.

01:23:51.950 --> 01:23:55.200
Does Carmen communicate
intelligently with other species?

01:23:55.200 --> 01:23:57.150
So we know you have cats, for instance.

01:23:57.150 --> 01:24:00.103
Does she talk with the cat,
to the cat, at the cat?

01:24:01.277 --> 01:24:03.030
- Well, she mimics the cat.

01:24:03.030 --> 01:24:08.030
So when she sees the cat, she does meow

01:24:08.077 --> 01:24:10.170
'cause she knows the cat's gonna meow.

01:24:10.170 --> 01:24:12.710
And as I said, she has this anticipatory

01:24:12.710 --> 01:24:14.010
relationship with life.

01:24:14.010 --> 01:24:14.843
But.

01:24:16.810 --> 01:24:19.730
One of the reasons that she's
not releasable to the wild,

01:24:19.730 --> 01:24:22.170
in addition to not knowing
how to fend for herself

01:24:22.170 --> 01:24:26.670
and not knowing the complicated
starling social networks,

01:24:26.670 --> 01:24:29.570
is that she thinks cats are her friends.

01:24:29.570 --> 01:24:33.120
So I don't let the cats out
when Carmen is flying around

01:24:33.120 --> 01:24:34.930
just because sometimes you can see

01:24:34.930 --> 01:24:36.570
the instinct coming over
them and they're just,

01:24:36.570 --> 01:24:38.950
the arm is, they know they're
not supposed to kill stuff

01:24:38.950 --> 01:24:39.940
that lives in the house.

01:24:39.940 --> 01:24:43.000
But sometimes the instinct is just,

01:24:43.000 --> 01:24:45.200
one of these days, it would arise.

01:24:45.200 --> 01:24:47.420
So the thing that does happen though,

01:24:47.420 --> 01:24:51.210
is when one of the cats goes
to the aviary and sits on it's,

01:24:51.210 --> 01:24:54.200
the aviary goes from the ceiling
all the way to the floor,

01:24:54.200 --> 01:24:56.400
and the cat will go up to the wire,

01:24:56.400 --> 01:24:59.410
put their noses right up to the wire,

01:24:59.410 --> 01:25:01.500
and Carmen will come and put her bill

01:25:01.500 --> 01:25:03.950
right next to their nose.

01:25:03.950 --> 01:25:05.060
Right next to it.

01:25:05.060 --> 01:25:06.420
And they'll maybe swat at her,

01:25:06.420 --> 01:25:09.550
and she just jumps back about two inches.

01:25:09.550 --> 01:25:12.960
And then, so they socialize together.

01:25:12.960 --> 01:25:15.130
I mean, I think the cats
just want to kill her,

01:25:15.130 --> 01:25:19.030
but Carmen thinks, oh,
it's like it's visiting me.

01:25:19.030 --> 01:25:20.930
Whenever anyone goes to Carmen's aviary,

01:25:20.930 --> 01:25:22.570
she just comes right up to see them

01:25:22.570 --> 01:25:24.830
and looks right into their
eyes and tilts their head.

01:25:24.830 --> 01:25:26.890
And she does that with the cats as well.

01:25:26.890 --> 01:25:28.530
- And she might perceive that she's safe

01:25:28.530 --> 01:25:31.250
thanks to the aviary enclosure too.

01:25:31.250 --> 01:25:34.700
- Oh, she does, but I don't
know what would happen if she,

01:25:34.700 --> 01:25:37.373
yeah, I don't trust her to know better.

01:25:37.373 --> 01:25:39.623
(laughing)

01:25:41.080 --> 01:25:41.913
- Thank you.

01:25:43.070 --> 01:25:46.620
Let's see, other questions.

01:25:46.620 --> 01:25:47.453
Did I miss any?

01:25:52.437 --> 01:25:53.610
We had people asking,

01:25:53.610 --> 01:25:57.590
can you purchase an adult
starling as Mozart did?

01:25:57.590 --> 01:25:58.640
Nope.

01:25:58.640 --> 01:26:01.340
And someone else already
said it would be illegal.

01:26:01.340 --> 01:26:03.920
Is that because they're non-native?

01:26:03.920 --> 01:26:05.390
- So it's actually interesting.

01:26:05.390 --> 01:26:08.800
I mean, I do not recommend
having a starling as a pet.

01:26:08.800 --> 01:26:11.240
They're very, very high
maintenance, takes a lot of work.

01:26:11.240 --> 01:26:12.750
But it is also illegal.

01:26:12.750 --> 01:26:15.300
In some states you are
able to get a license

01:26:15.300 --> 01:26:16.540
to have one as a pet.

01:26:16.540 --> 01:26:18.640
When I talked to our own

01:26:18.640 --> 01:26:21.415
Washington State Fish and Wildlife person

01:26:21.415 --> 01:26:26.415
and asked why we are
allowed to, with impunity,

01:26:27.000 --> 01:26:31.960
kill adult starlings
without telling anyone,

01:26:31.960 --> 01:26:34.770
it's fine, it's legal,
it's even encouraged,

01:26:34.770 --> 01:26:37.410
why can we not just have one in the house?

01:26:37.410 --> 01:26:39.840
And he said that they
are worried about people

01:26:39.840 --> 01:26:41.740
propagating them as pets.

01:26:41.740 --> 01:26:44.270
And it's kind of a silly worry

01:26:44.270 --> 01:26:47.630
because there are so many starlings

01:26:47.630 --> 01:26:49.830
and we kill lots of starlings.

01:26:49.830 --> 01:26:54.090
We kill millions of starlings a year

01:26:54.090 --> 01:26:57.380
for agricultural protection.

01:26:57.380 --> 01:27:01.430
But it doesn't make a dent
in the starling population.

01:27:01.430 --> 01:27:03.170
And so someone would have to release

01:27:03.170 --> 01:27:04.880
a heck of a lot of starlings

01:27:04.880 --> 01:27:06.970
in order for it to make any difference.

01:27:06.970 --> 01:27:08.220
And it's hard to imagine what

01:27:08.220 --> 01:27:09.590
the motive for doing that would be.

01:27:09.590 --> 01:27:12.160
So I'm not saying that
I think it's a good idea

01:27:12.160 --> 01:27:15.460
for people to have pet starlings,

01:27:15.460 --> 01:27:19.210
but I don't think it makes
sense for us to be able to

01:27:19.210 --> 01:27:22.360
kill them or whatever but
not keep them as pets.

01:27:22.360 --> 01:27:26.770
But there is no legal
starling trade out there.

01:27:26.770 --> 01:27:28.750
You'll have to, you're on your own,

01:27:28.750 --> 01:27:30.300
if you want to get a starling.

01:27:30.300 --> 01:27:33.070
- I thought it was especially
interesting in your book

01:27:33.070 --> 01:27:37.410
how you delved into the rather brutal ways

01:27:37.410 --> 01:27:40.890
in which it's open season on starlings

01:27:41.760 --> 01:27:44.320
and the various, I remember,

01:27:44.320 --> 01:27:47.440
some of the harrowing
stories you told of people

01:27:47.440 --> 01:27:49.440
doing their bit, I suppose,

01:27:49.440 --> 01:27:53.310
to protect native species by
killing off the starlings.

01:27:53.310 --> 01:27:57.250
And yet one of the things I
really gathered from your talk

01:27:57.250 --> 01:28:00.010
and your book was how doing so,

01:28:00.010 --> 01:28:04.320
and such a focus on eradicating them,

01:28:04.320 --> 01:28:09.200
inures us to their own special nature.

01:28:09.200 --> 01:28:12.450
And this greater intelligence
that we can gather

01:28:12.450 --> 01:28:13.750
and immerse ourselves in

01:28:13.750 --> 01:28:17.960
of these animals that are
otherwise unwelcome here.

01:28:17.960 --> 01:28:20.170
They did come here at our own hand.

01:28:20.170 --> 01:28:21.520
It was the one who brought them over,

01:28:21.520 --> 01:28:25.350
releasing them in the part
of the Shakespearian story.

01:28:25.350 --> 01:28:28.000
And it taught me to look, for instance,

01:28:28.000 --> 01:28:31.296
at the house sparrows outside nesting.

01:28:31.296 --> 01:28:34.130
They have a beauty and a
rhythm of their own life

01:28:34.130 --> 01:28:35.730
and that has value too.

01:28:35.730 --> 01:28:37.510
So thank you for that.

01:28:37.510 --> 01:28:41.570
- Right, I mean, one of the
things is it's not either or.

01:28:41.570 --> 01:28:43.480
One of the things that's
so beautiful about

01:28:43.480 --> 01:28:45.790
the complexity of human intelligence

01:28:45.790 --> 01:28:48.290
is that we can hold both of
these things at one time.

01:28:48.290 --> 01:28:50.370
We can dislike starlings ecologically,

01:28:50.370 --> 01:28:53.200
we can understand that their
presence is an ecological harm,

01:28:53.200 --> 01:28:56.430
and yet we can be inspired or

01:28:57.550 --> 01:29:00.950
by the interconnection that
their consciousness invites

01:29:00.950 --> 01:29:04.600
and just by their, even
just by their beauty.

01:29:04.600 --> 01:29:07.418
So it's not one or the other.

01:29:07.418 --> 01:29:09.760
We can do all of these things at once.

01:29:09.760 --> 01:29:11.810
And that's one of the things I tried to do

01:29:11.810 --> 01:29:14.943
with this research was
invite that kind of nuance.

01:29:20.250 --> 01:29:21.580
- Great.

01:29:21.580 --> 01:29:23.310
I guess, Rick, do you have any closing

01:29:23.310 --> 01:29:25.237
questions of your own?

01:29:25.237 --> 01:29:27.900
- No, no, I enjoyed this thoroughly

01:29:27.900 --> 01:29:32.900
and I'm fascinated to see this
new angle on understanding

01:29:33.963 --> 01:29:37.480
bird intelligence and
also the history of others

01:29:37.480 --> 01:29:39.840
who appreciate it as well.

01:29:39.840 --> 01:29:41.697
So thanks very much.

01:29:42.810 --> 01:29:44.320
- Yeah, so at this point,

01:29:44.320 --> 01:29:48.530
I want to say a big thank
you to Lyanda and to Rick

01:29:48.530 --> 01:29:52.440
for this wonderful, wonderful event.

01:29:52.440 --> 01:29:55.580
And we are wishing you the
best for your new book.

01:29:55.580 --> 01:29:59.420
And can't wait to read that too.

01:29:59.420 --> 01:30:01.720
And continue to have fun with Carmen.

01:30:01.720 --> 01:30:04.703
It was so nice and generous
of you to share her

01:30:06.290 --> 01:30:08.050
sort of flying around

01:30:08.050 --> 01:30:13.050
and I'm glad she decided to
participate in today's session.

01:30:13.201 --> 01:30:15.347
- Me too. (laughs)

01:30:15.347 --> 01:30:16.180
I want to thank everyone.

01:30:16.180 --> 01:30:19.870
Thank you for bearing with
the technological rockiness

01:30:19.870 --> 01:30:22.650
and also just the sort of awkwardness

01:30:22.650 --> 01:30:26.890
of the talking head way that this platform

01:30:26.890 --> 01:30:28.760
requires us to communicate.

01:30:28.760 --> 01:30:32.200
But the Q&amp;A is always the most fun

01:30:32.200 --> 01:30:35.570
and I'm just delighted
to be part of the series.

01:30:35.570 --> 01:30:38.220
Again, I want to thank
everyone so much for having me

01:30:38.220 --> 01:30:39.790
and for listening.

01:30:39.790 --> 01:30:41.910
And Professor Rick, thank you.

01:30:41.910 --> 01:30:43.260
You're a delight.

01:30:43.260 --> 01:30:44.360
I'm a fan of your work

01:30:44.360 --> 01:30:47.510
and it was so fun to be here with you.

01:30:47.510 --> 01:30:51.680
- Yeah, so thank you everyone
for attending and talked.

01:30:51.680 --> 01:30:54.230
Thank you so much, Ty Kamp

01:30:54.230 --> 01:30:57.370
for helping making it
all go very smoothly.

01:30:57.370 --> 01:30:58.623
So bye, everyone.

